ed topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are not surface anaesthetics"},{"label":"B","text":"Have a shorter duration of action"},{"label":"C","text":"Are degraded in the plasma"},{"label":"D","text":"Do not show cross-sensitivity with ester- linked local anaesthetics (p. 321)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-63f080f1b316","stem":"3 The following is not true of local anaesthetics","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The local anaesthetic is required in the unionized form for penetrating the neuronal membrane"},{"label":"B","text":"The local anaesthetic approaches its receptor only from the intraneuronal face of the Na+ channel"},{"label":"C","text":"The local anaesthetic binds to its receptor mainly when the Na+ channel is in the resting state"},{"label":"D","text":"The local anaesthetic combines with its receptor in the ionized cationic form (p. 321-322)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4031f89bcf16","stem":"4 Local anaesthetics block nerve conduction by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blocking all cation channels in the neuro- nal membrane"},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpolarizing the neuronal membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"Interfering with depolarization of the neuro- nal membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"Both ‘B’ and ‘C’ are correct (p. 321-322)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-075ad3e08599","stem":"5 Sensitivity of a nerve fibre to blockade by lignocaine depends on","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whether the fibre is sensory or motor"},{"label":"B","text":"Whether the fibre is myelinated or nonmyeli-Aim4aiims.in nated"},{"label":"C","text":"Internodal distances in the fibre"},{"label":"D","text":"Both ‘B’ and ‘C’ are correct (p. 322-323) 22222 44444 ..... 22222 D 22222 44444 ..... 33333 C 22222 44444 ..... 44444 C 22222 44444 ..... 55555 D Peripheral Nervous System 155155155155155"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-7473083eb8b4","stem":"6 A resting nerve is relatively resistant to blockade by lignocaine compared to one which is repeatedly stimulated because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lignocaine penetrates resting nerve memb- rane poorly"},{"label":"B","text":"Lignocaine binds more avidly to the inactiva- ted Na+ channel"},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve impulse promotes ionization of lignocaine"},{"label":"D","text":"Nodes of Ranvier are inaccessible in the resting state (p. 321-322)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-77ec203b9fff","stem":"7 Which of the following is not the reason for greater susceptibility of smaller sensory fibres to blockade by local anaesthetics than larger motor fibres","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensory fibres are inherently more sensitive than motor fibres"},{"label":"B","text":"More slender fibres have shorter internodal distances"},{"label":"C","text":"Small sensory fibres generate higher frequency longer lasting action potential"},{"label":"D","text":"Smaller fibres have shorter critical lengths for blockade (p. 323)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0ff40aac83fc","stem":"8 Which sensation is blocked first by low concentra- tions of a local anaesthetic","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain"},{"label":"B","text":"Temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"Touch"},{"label":"D","text":"Deep pressure (p. 323)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-07ef8a6dfda6","stem":"9 Injection of adrenaline along with a local anaesthetic serves the following purpose","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lowers the concentration of the local anaes- thetic to produce nerve block"},{"label":"B","text":"Prolongs the duration of local anaesthesiaAim4aiims.in"},{"label":"C","text":"Increases the anaesthetised area"},{"label":"D","text":"Reduces the local toxicity of the local anaes- thetic (p. 323) 22222 44444 ..... 66666 B 22222 44444 ..... 77777 A 22222 44444 ..... 88888 A 22222 44444 ..... 99999 B 156156156156156 MCQs in Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6cb1ea5576cf","stem":"10 Adrenaline added to local anaesthetic solution for infiltration anaesthesia affords the following except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolongs the duration of local anaesthesia"},{"label":"B","text":"Makes the injection less painful"},{"label":"C","text":"Provides a more bloodless field for surgery"},{"label":"D","text":"Reduces systemic toxicity of the local anaes- thetic (p. 323)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e151cc314bc1","stem":"11 The following local anaesthetic raises BP instead of tending to cause a fall","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cocaine"},{"label":"B","text":"Dibucaine"},{"label":"C","text":"Lignocaine"},{"label":"D","text":"Procaine (p. 324)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-880a2b2003d2","stem":"12 Toxicity of local anaesthetics involves the following organs except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart"},{"label":"B","text":"Brain"},{"label":"C","text":"Kidney"},{"label":"D","text":"Skin and subcutaneous tissue (p. 324)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_g