4 ..... 22222 66666 C 22222 44444 ..... 22222 77777 A 22222 44444 ..... 22222 88888 D"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-72c1d1b2143b","stem":"When two drugs interact with the same receptor (same pharmacologic mechanism), the D-R Efficacy: the maximal effect an curves will have parallel slopes. Drugs A and B have the same mechanism; drugs X and Y do agonist can achieve at the not. highest practical concentration. Notice the analogy with the","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-00f796576420","stem":"Which one of the following routes of drug administration produces the most rapid absorption?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhalation"},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous"},{"label":"C","text":"Oral"},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal"},{"label":"E","text":"Sublingual"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-df5c783b347d","stem":"A patient who experiences migraines has accidentally overdosed with methysergide, a weak base of pKa = 6.5. If urinary pH in this patient is 5.5, which of the following state- ments regarding elimination of methysergide from the body is accurate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in urinary pH will increase excretion rate."},{"label":"B","text":"Urinary excretion is already maximal, and changes in pH will have no effect."},{"label":"C","text":"Attempts should be made to acidify the urine to at least 4 units below drug pKa."},{"label":"D","text":"At a urinary pH of 5.5, methysergide is 99% ionized."},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-670f3cc946ea","stem":"A patient was given a 160-mg dose of a drug IV, and 80 mg was eliminated during the first 120 minutes. If the drug follows first-order elimination kinetics, how much of the drug will remain 6 hours after its administration? percentage of filtered drug must be reabsorbed in the renal tubules? obtain an initial plasma level of 10 mg/L in a patient weighing 70 kg? Which one of the following is a phase I drug metabolism reaction? the formation of hepatic microsomal drug-metabolizing enzymes?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"None"},{"label":"B","text":"10mg"},{"label":"C","text":"20mg"},{"label":"D","text":"40 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"60 mg KAPLAN' 28 medical Review Questions -- A subject in whom the renal clearance of inulin is 120 mllmin is given a drug, the clear- ance of which is found to be 18 mllmin. If the drug is 40% plasma protein bound, what"},{"label":"A","text":"None"},{"label":"B","text":"12.5"},{"label":"C","text":"25 If a drug is known to be distributed into total body water, what dose (mg) is needed to"},{"label":"A","text":"420"},{"label":"B","text":"300"},{"label":"C","text":"210"},{"label":"D","text":"100"},{"label":"E","text":"70"},{"label":"A","text":"Acetylation"},{"label":"B","text":"Glucuronidation"},{"label":"C","text":"Methylation"},{"label":"D","text":"Reduction"},{"label":"E","text":"Sulfation With chronic administration, which one of the following drugs is LEAST likely to induce"},{"label":"A","text":"Carbamazepine"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanol"},{"label":"C","text":"Ketoconazole"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenobarbital"},{"label":"E","text":"Rifampin medicalKAPLAN' 29 USMLE Step 1: Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels","duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_labels","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a2f3907a27fd","stem":"A 500-mg dose of a drug has therapeutic efficacy for 6 h. If the half-life of the drug is 8 h, for how long would a 1-g dose be effective?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 h"},{"label":"B","text":"12 h"},{"label":"C","text":"14 h"},{"label":"D","text":"16h"},{"label":"E","text":"24h"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-81c55909aaf7","stem":"A drug achieves a plasma level of 16 mg/L shortly after the administration of the first oral dose. If the half-life and the dosing interval are both 6 h, what is the approximate plasma level shortly before the administration of the 5th dose?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-da990e3f8c19","stem":"In the case of a drug that follows first order elimination, lowing statements is accurate? equivalent to a steady-state level of 20 pg/L?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"the rate of elimination is constant"},{"label":"B","text":"the elimination half-life varies with the dose"},{"label":"C","text":"the volume of distribution varies with the dose"},{"label":"D","text":"the clearance varies with the dose"},{"label":"E","text":"the rate of elimination varies directly with the dose KAPLAN' 30 medical Review Questions -- --- The curves in this figure represent isolated tissue responses to two drugs. Which of the fol- Log Dose Figure 1-2-8"},{"label":"A","text":"Drug A has greater efficacy than drug B"},{"label":"B","text":"Drug A is more potent than drug B"},{"label":"C","text":"Drug B is more potent than drug A"},{"label":"D","text":"Drug B has greater efficacy than drug A"},{"label":"E","text":"Both drugs have the same affinity In a patient weighing 70 kg, acetaminophen has a Vd = 70 L and CL = 350 mL/min. The elimination half-life of the drug is approximately"},{"label":"A","text":"35 rnin"},{"label":"B","text":"70 rnin"},{"label":"C","text":"140 rnin"},{"label":"D","text":"210min"},{"label":"E","text":"280 rnin Pharmacokinetic characteristics of propranolol include Vd = 300 L170 kg, CL = 700 mLImin, oral bioavailability f = 0.25. What is the dose needed to achieve a plasma level With IV infusion, a drug reaches 90% of its final steady state in 10 hours. The elimination half-life of the drug must be approximately"},{"label":"A","text":"1 h"},{"label":"B","text":"2 h"},{"label":"C","text":"3 h"},{"label":"D","text":"6 h"},{"label":"E","text":"9 h medicalKAPLAN' 31 pppp---USMLE Step 1: Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1e91cbe9dc48","stem":"At 12 h after IV administration of a bolus dose, the plasma level of a drug is 3 mg/L. If the Vd = 10 L and the elimination half-life = 6 h, what was the dose administered?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"120 mg"},{"label":"B","text":"180 mg"},{"label":"C","text":"240 mg"},{"label":"D","text":"480 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"600 mg"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c2a0efaa2449","stem":"An 1V infusion of a drug is started at 400 mglh. If CL = 50 Llh, what is the anticipated plasma level at steady state?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"2mglL"},{"label":"B","text":"4 mg/L"},{"label":"C","text":"8 mg/L"},{"label":"D","text":"16 mg/L"},{"label":"E","text":"32 mgJL 32 medicalKAPLAN' Review Questions Answers . Aspirin (pKa = 3.5) is absorbed quite readily from the stomach. At a stomach pH of 1.5 (pH - pKa = -2), 99% of aspirin molecules are in the unionized form. Remember, unionized molecular forms of drugs are capable of permeation through biomembranes- ionized forms are \"trapped\". . The key word is absorption. By definition, IV injection does not involve absorption processes because drug is injected directly into the systemic circulation (choice B is wrong). All of the other modes of administration are associated with absorption. The inhalational mode is the most rapid because of the great area of the absorptive surface and the close proximity to the blood. . Drugs with extensive plasma protein binding usually have low Vd values (recall Vd = dose/CO)and slow renal elimination because only the free fraction is filtered. Albumin is the major plasma protein to which drugs bind, and the constant positive charge on quaternary amines prevents their binding to plasma proteins. Competition between drugs for plasma protein binding sites can lead to drug interactions (e.g., the dis- placement of warfarin by sulfonamides may increase its anticoagulant effects). . The permeation of most drugs through cellular membranes is by the process of passive diffusion, a nonsaturable process that follows first-order kinetics. Concentration gradient and lipid solubility of the drug are important determinants of the rate of diffu- sion. Only a few drug molecules are substrates for active transport processes (eg., tubular secretion of beta-lactam antibiotics); these are saturable at high concentrations. Only very small ions (e.g., Li') or drugs (e.g., ethanol) may penetrate bioinembranes via aqueous pores. . Weak bases are more readily excreted in the urine when pH < pKa, so urinary acidification can often be of value in overdose situations (choice A is wrong). With just one unit of pH difference from pKa, excretion is not maximal for methysergide, in that 10% of the drug molecules would be in nonionized form capable of tubular reabsorption (choic- es B and D are wrong). Acidification of the urine to a pH of 2.5 (4 pH units < pKa) is not compatible with life (choice C is wrong). . One half of the drug dose is eliminated in 120 min, so its elimination half-life = 2 hours. With the passage of each half-life, the amount in the body (or in the blood) will decrease to 50% of a former level. Thus, at 6 hours after drug administration, the amount of drug remaining is 160 divided by (2 x 2 x 2) or 16018 = 20 mg. . The formula to use is CL = ff x GFR. From the question, ff = 0.6 (40% bound) and GFR = 120 mL/min, so the product (apparent CL) = 72 mL/min. However, the ques- tion states that the actual CL = 18 mL/min; thus, the difference from apparent CL is 54 mL/min (72 - 18), and this represents the amount of drug reabsorbed following its glomerular filtration. Percentage reabsorption = 54/72 x 100 = 75%. . This is a \"loading dose\" question. Remember LD = Vd x CO. In this case, Vd = 42 L, which approximates total body water in a patient weighing 70 kg. LD = 42 L x 10 mg/L = 420 mg USMLE Step 1: Pharmacology --"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a9659d8ade5a","stem":"Which one of the following is a phase I drug metabolism reaction?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-31b6d10bb953","stem":"Which of the following statements is most correct regarding Time (h) the termination of drug action?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f12fe6f1e1a4b2cb738a1e45ab31074804567bad"},{"id":"q-57481d73e0be","stem":"Questions that deal with acid-base (Henderson-Hasselbalch)7. Which statement about the distribution of drugs to specific manipulations are common on examinations. Since absorp- tissues is most correct?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ea2059e4ae21","stem":"If you wish to give Mr Jones an intravenous loading dose Dosage = 100100 mg/dg/d (li(liver)e ) + 100 mg/d to achieve the therapeutic plasma concentration of 4 mg/L 20 mL/min rapidly, how much should be given?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-599db112c732","stem":"A 63-year-old woman in the intensive care unit requires an 4 2.0 infusion of procainamide. Its half-life is 2 h. The infusion is begun at 9 AM. At 1 PM on the same day, a blood sample is 8 3.0 taken; the drug concentration is found to be 3 mg/L. What 10 3.6 is the probable steady-state drug concentration, for example, after 12 or more hours of infusion?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2946c4d08668","stem":"Which of the following drugs may inhibit the hepatic micro-(eg, acetylcysteine) may be life-saving after an overdose. In severe somal P450 responsible for warfarin metabolism? liver disease, stores of glucuronide, sulfate, and glutathione may (A) Amiodarone be depleted, making the patient more susceptible to hepatic toxic- (B) Ethanol ity with near-normal doses of acetaminophen. Enzyme inducers (C) Phenobarbital (eg, ethanol) may increase acetaminophen toxicity because they (D) Procainamide increase phase I metabolism more than phase II metabolism, thus (E) Rifampin resulting in increased production of the reactive metabolite. 7. Which of the following drugs, if used chronically, is most likely to increase the toxicity of acetaminophen? (A) Cimetidine QUESTIONS (B) Ethanol (C) Ketoconazole1–2. You are planning to treat asthma in a 19-year-old patient (D) Procainamidewith recurrent, episodic attacks of bronchospasm with wheezing. (E) QuinidineYou are concerned about drug interactions caused by changes in (F) Ritonavirdrug metabolism in this patient. (G) Succinylcholine","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f527b0c835b85d49aa90603ea1b34ec4c8f5f7ae"},{"id":"q-9989ac2fce1f","stem":"Drug metabolism in humans usually results in a product that is (H) Verapamil (A) Less lipid soluble than the original drug 8. Which of the following drugs has higher first-pass metabolism (B) More likely to distribute intracellularly in men than in women?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d4d9aea3ba8c","stem":"If therapy with multiple drugs causes induction of drug (D) Procainamide metabolism in your asthma patient, it will (E) Quinidine (A) Be associated with increased smooth endoplasmic reticulum (F) Ritonavir (B) Be associated with increased rough endoplasmic reticulum (G) Succinylcholine (C) Be associated with decreased enzymes in the soluble cyto- (H) Verapamil plasmic fraction 9. Which of the following drugs is an established inhibitor of (D) Require 3–4 months to reach completion P-glycoprotein (P-gp) drug transporters?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c9a5aacc8f3a","stem":"Which of the following factors is likely to increase the duration (B) Ethanol of action of a drug that is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the (C) Ketoconazole liver? (D) Procainamide (A) Chronic administration of phenobarbital before and (E) Quinidine during therapy with the drug in question (F) Ritonavir (B) Chronic therapy with cimetidine (G) Succinylcholine (C) Displacement from tissue-binding sites by another drug (H) Verapamil (D) Increased cardiac output 10. Which of the following agents, when used in combination (E) Chronic administration of rifampin with other anti-HIV drugs, permits dose reductions?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-964dda55830a","stem":"2: Mr. Kimathi is admitted to General Hospital with pneumonia due to gram-negative bacteria. The antibiotic tobramycin is ordered. The CL and Vd of tobramycin in Mr. Kimathi are 80 mL/min and 40 L, respectively. 1. What maintenance dose should be administered intravenously every 6 hours to eventually obtain average steady-state plasma concentrations of 4 mg/L? (A) 0.32 mg (B) 19.2 mg (C) 115 mg (D) 160 mg (E) 230 mg 2. If you wish to give Mr. Kimathi an IV loading dose to achieve the therapeutic plasma concentration of 4 mg/L rapidly, how much should be given? (A) 0.1 mg (B) 10 mg (C) 115.2 mg (D) 160 mg [E) None of the above","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"4th November 2009"},{"label":"B","text":"Pharmacokinetics -Session ll"},{"label":"C","text":"Exercise 4"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8c6ac6efdb53","stem":"What is clinical pharmacokinetics ?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"91474a0009d32702990ebd6fa126aa1da0b26ec8"},{"id":"q-dce4a79aaf36","stem":"What is Steady State (SS) ?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-547bd3f73fe5","stem":"Why is it important ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"4th November 2009"},{"label":"B","text":"Pharmacokinetics -Session ll"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd31511aa067","stem":"What is clinical pharmacokinetics ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharmacokinetics (PK) & pharmacodynamics (PD)"},{"label":"B","text":"Pharmacokinetics (PK) and pharmacodynamics (PD)"},{"label":"C","text":"Pharmacokinetic techniques"},{"label":"D","text":"Pharmacokinetic techniques"},{"label":"E","text":"Pharmacokinetic parameters"},{"label":"F","text":"Slide 9"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"91474a0009d32702990ebd6fa126aa1da0b26ec8"},{"id":"q-7cb5f705fa9b","stem":"What is Steady State (SS) ? Why is it important ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elimination half-life (t1/2)"},{"label":"B","text":"Use of t1/2:"},{"label":"C","text":"Slide 45"},{"label":"D","text":"Use of t1/2"},{"label":"E","text":"Slide 47"},{"label":"F","text":"Slide 48"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-76da2a8937b8","stem":"2005 DR Complex Effect [Slide 34] Drug (D) - Receptor (R) Interaction Kd = ([D] * [R]) / [DR] = k-1/k1 k1 k-1 D + R DR Kd = dissociation constant k1 = association rate constant K-1= dissociation rate constant [Slide 35] Assessment of Receptor Occupation Measure of Affinitykd is a relative measure of affinity of a drug for its receptor. It varies inversely with the affinity of the drug for its receptor High-affinity drugs have lower kd values and occupy a greater number of drug receptors than drugs with lower affinities. [Slide 36] What can we learn?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7a04f6cf95e3ca530ca3cc4522849652117354f6"},{"id":"q-6d2209339d6b","stem":"0 10 1000 [Slide 102] Practice Question In the absence of other drugs, pindolol causes an increase in heart rate by activating beta adrenoceptors. In the presence of highly effective beta stimulants, however, pindolol causes a dose-dependent, reversible decrease in heart rate. Therefore, pindolol is probably An irreversible antagonist A physiologic antagonist A chemical antagonist A partial agonist A spare receptor agonist [Slide 103] Practice question Which line is most efficacious? Which is more potent?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8bfb350fc8b3","stem":"How does all this happen?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacodynamic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6167f37fefd8","stem":"Why might this be a problem for someone with asthma and hypertension?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Hypertension","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0924f29f747f","stem":"What can we learn?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f9c0cd3cf643","stem":"Which of the following statements correctly pair the adrenergic drug with the receptor(s) that are mainly involved in its effects? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epinephrine - beta-1, D1"},{"label":"B","text":"Norepinephrine -Alpha-1, alpha-2, beta-1"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol - alpha-1, beta-1"},{"label":"D","text":"Dobutamine - D1, D2"},{"label":"E","text":"Clonidine - Beta-1, Beta-2"},{"label":"F","text":"Albuterol - Beta-2"},{"label":"G","text":"Phenylephrine - alpha-1"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-03c2e1040071","stem":"The effects of epinephrine on the heart are most likely mediated by which of the following postreceptor mechanisms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of ligand gated K+ channels"},{"label":"B","text":"Opening of ligand-gated Na+ channels"},{"label":"C","text":"Simulation of phospholipase C"},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulation of phospholipase A2"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulation of adenylyl cyclase"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-581c67b8824f","stem":"Which of the following actions best explains the decrease in heart rate observed with norepinephrine infusion in a healthy subject?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of myocardial alpha-2 receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulation of aortic baroreceptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Activation of Nm receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"Blockade of myocardial muscarinic receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulation of chemoreceptors of aortic bodies"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d738e481d012","stem":"Which of the following are cardiovascular effects of epinephrine? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased A-V conduction"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased cutaneous blood flow"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased arterial and venous pulmonary pressure"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased renal blood flow"},{"label":"E","text":"Decreased blood flow in the skeletal muscle"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-191f4ae2d655","stem":"Which of the following is the most common adverse effect of phenylephrine given locally into the eye for diagnostic purposes?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lacrimation"},{"label":"B","text":"Tachycardia"},{"label":"C","text":"Difficulty in far vision"},{"label":"D","text":"Photophobia"},{"label":"E","text":"Running nose"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-90ba469bc667","stem":"Which of the following is a pharmacological effect of isoproterenol?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulation of renin secretion"},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of insulin release"},{"label":"C","text":"Reflex bradycardia"},{"label":"D","text":"Release of inflammatory mediators from mast cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulation of platelet aggregation"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-29f012a4300d","stem":"Which of the following is a pharmacological effect of albuterol on the respiratory system?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased microvascular permeability"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased bronchial secretions"},{"label":"C","text":"Release of histamine from mast cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased residual respiratory volume"},{"label":"E","text":"Increased smooth muscle relaxation"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a36657ac6b98","stem":"Which of the following is a common adverse effect of selective beta-2 receptor agonists?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-V block"},{"label":"B","text":"Tremor"},{"label":"C","text":"Sleepiness"},{"label":"D","text":"Postural hypotension"},{"label":"E","text":"Ventricular tachycardia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5119feac47b","stem":"Which of the following statements correctly pairs the adverse effects of beta-2 selective adrenergic agonists with the action that best explains that effect? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sinus tachycardia - reflex stimulation of vagus nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"Flushing -peripheral vasodilation"},{"label":"C","text":"Tremors -activation of beta-2 receptors in basal ganglia"},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperglycemia -increased gluconeogenesis"},{"label":"E","text":"Hypokalemia -increased K+ elimination by the kidney"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4d8fd98706d2","stem":"Which of the following autonomic drugs given IV to healthy subjects can cause an increase in cardiac contractility, a slight increase in heart rate and no major changes or a decrease in systemic vascular resistance?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylephrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"D","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Dobutamine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ce86e8a64914","stem":"Which of the following statements best explains the main mechanism of adrenergic actions of cocaine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of monoamine oxidase A"},{"label":"B","text":"Blockade of alpha-2 receptors in sympathetic terminals"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of norepinephrine reuptake into the sympathetic nerve terminals"},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulation of epinephrine release from the adrenal medulla"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulation of norepinephrine release from the sympathetic nerve terminals"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0a64b4494ccb","stem":"Which of the following statements correctly pairs the adrenergic agonist with its appropriate therapeutic use?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ephedrine - insomnia"},{"label":"B","text":"Dobutamine - anaphylactic shock"},{"label":"C","text":"Norepinephrine - neurogenic migraine"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine - nasal congestion"},{"label":"E","text":"Albuterol- exertional angina"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-310a6bfbc679","stem":"A 12-year-old boy was stung by a wasp while walking in the woods. A few minutes later he developed pruritus and erythema, a generalized feeling of warm and impending doom, dyspnea, wheezing and dizziness. Increasing swelling of the face, eyes, lips and tongue was prominent. Paramedics were called and they administered a subcutaneous drug upon arrival. Which of the following drugs was most likely administered?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"C","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Ephedrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenylephrine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dc4faf6370c0","stem":"A 58-year-old man underwent surgery for the removal of a necrotic bowel tissue. Five days later he became confused and disoriented. Physical finding included fever of 103°F, blood pressure of 90/50 mm Hg,pulse 130 bpm, respiratory rate 30 breath/min. Pertinent lab values were PaCO2 30 mm Hg (normal 33-45), HCO3- 18 mEq/L (normal 22-28), serum pH 7.31, WBC count 22,000 cells/mm3. Arterial and pulmonary artery catheters were inserted, revealing a cardiac output of 6 L/min and a systemic vascular resistance of 610 dyne∙sec∙cm-5 (normal 800-1400). A fluid therapy was initiated but one hour later the mean blood pressure was still 60 mm Hg. An IV infusion of which of the following drugs would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Albuterol"},{"label":"E","text":"clonidine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1fc024e50142","stem":"A 56-year-old woman suffering from idiopathic orthostatic hypotension is brought unconscious to the emergency department. She regains consciousness after a supportive treatment and an IV administration of phenylephrine. Which of the following actions most likely account for the therapeutic effectiveness of the drug in the present case?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilation of cerebral vessels"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased heart rate"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased cardiac output"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased systemic blood pressure"},{"label":"E","text":"Increased levels of angiotensin II"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3b74fc446ee6","stem":"0 Ejection fraction 60% 60% The effects of the new drug are most similar to those of which of the following agents?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dobutamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"D","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenylephrine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb693870cd08","stem":"A 52-year-old woman has been recently diagnosed with severe bronchial asthma and an appropriate treatment was prescribed. Two weeks into therapy she complained of tremors, muscle cramping, palpitations and anxiety. Which of the following drugs most likely caused the symptoms in this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"B","text":"Albuterol"},{"label":"C","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Dobutamine"},{"label":"E","text":"Prazosin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-caeeb3ce4ae0","stem":"A 6-year-old girl presents to the family physician in company of her mother, who was distraught over the teacher’s comment that the child was “unmanageable” at school. The teacher referred that the girl is unable to remain seated for the duration of a structured activity and to listen when spoken to directly. She also often fidgets with hands and feet, squirms and talks excessively. Similar symptoms are noted during the doctor’s visit. Which of the following drugs would be appropriate to treat this condition?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylephrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Albuterol"},{"label":"D","text":"Amphetamine"},{"label":"E","text":"Norepinephrine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-87bca2dc838e","stem":"5 From the above data the physician concludes that there is a localized lesion in which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postganglionic sympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"B","text":"Preganglionic sympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"C","text":"Postganglionic parasympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"D","text":"Preganglionic parasympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"E","text":"Medulla oblongata"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-822346c91ce1","stem":"Which of the following statements correctly pairs the adrenoreceptor blocker with the type of blockade it provokes? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metoprolol - irreversible, beta-1 selective"},{"label":"B","text":"Prazosin - reversible, alpha-2 selective"},{"label":"C","text":"Labetalol - irreversible, non-selective"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenoxybenzamine - irreversible, alpha non-selective"},{"label":"E","text":"Propranolol - reversible, beta non-selective"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8c5d311046c7","stem":"Which of the following two actions best explains the phenoxybenzamine-induced increase in heart rate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased firing rates of carotid baroreceptors / direct release of norepinephrine from cardiac adrenergic terminals"},{"label":"B","text":"Coronary vasodilation / blockade of acetylcholine release from cardiac cholinergic terminals"},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary vasodilation / direct release of norepinephrine from cardiac adrenergic terminals"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased firing rates of carotid baroreceptors / blockade of acetylcholine release from cardiac cholinergic terminals"},{"label":"E","text":"Decreased firing rates of carotid baroreceptors / blockade of muscarinic receptors in the heart"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2e3f919471ad","stem":"Which of the following are pharmacological effects of alpha-1 receptor antagonists? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reversal of epinephrine-induced hypertension"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased skin blood flow"},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased heart rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Contraction of trigone and bladder internal sphincter"},{"label":"E","text":"Relaxation of the radial muscle of iris."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-172d6b64e003","stem":"In an experimental animal model, acute prazosin pretreatment will most effectively counteract which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoproterenol-induced tachycardia"},{"label":"B","text":"Nicotine-induced sweating"},{"label":"C","text":"dopamine-induced increase in renal blood flow"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine-induced hypertension"},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine-induced contraction of ciliary muscle"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3d91fa807f4b","stem":"In an experimental animal model, an acute propranolol pretreatment will most effectively counteract which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoproterenol-induced hyperglycemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Nicotine-induced decrease in skin blood flow"},{"label":"C","text":"Norepinephrine-induced reflex bradycardia"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine-induced mydriasis"},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine-induced contraction of bronchial musculature"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dcf4f115209f","stem":"Which of the following statements correctly pair the beta-blocker with its appropriate therapeutic indication? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metoprolol - exertional angina"},{"label":"B","text":"Esmolol - long-term control of hypertension"},{"label":"C","text":"Timolol - hyperlipidemic states"},{"label":"D","text":"Propranolol - migraine"},{"label":"E","text":"Carvedilol -hypovolemic states"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-50fe1bf7fdbf","stem":"Which of the following are metabolic effects of non-selective beta blockers?(Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased lipolysis"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased renin release"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased hepatic glycogenolysis"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased HDL"},{"label":"E","text":"Enhancement of insulin-induced hypoglycemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05fa2fc4ec23","stem":"Which of the following pairs of autonomic drugs is a correct receptor agonist-antagonist match? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoproterenol - prazosin"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine -carvedilol"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylephrine - atropine"},{"label":"D","text":"Albuterol - propranolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Nicotine - dopamine"},{"label":"F","text":"Norepinephrine - clonidine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cd271defd0fb","stem":"A 75-year-old man, suffering from congestive heart failure, has been recently diagnosed with benign prostatic hyperplasia. Since surgical ablation of the prostate is contraindicated, a drug suitable for the treatment of patient’s impaired bladder emptying is prescribed. Which of the following statements best explains the molecular mechanism of action of that drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blockade of beta-2 receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Activation of beta-2 receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Activation of alpha-2 receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"Blockade of alpha-1 receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Activation of M3 receptors"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-76dae007197c","stem":"A 57-year-old woman recently diagnosed with pheochromocytoma was under surgery to remove the tumor. With manipulation of the adrenal mass her blood pressure rose to 200/140mm Hg. Which of the following pairs of drugs were most likely administered during the operation to reduce her blood pressure?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenoxybenzamine and propranolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Prazosin and esmolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Metyrosine and pindolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Clonidine and propranolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Clonidine and pindolol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5a0b72a9a19562130aec6b3fbcf2a293f78b9e2c"},{"id":"q-eb75ded8af2b","stem":"A 65-year-old man has been suffering from essential tremor for many years. The disorder consists of a fine, slow tremor of the hands brought out by performing skilled acts. Now the patient comes to his doctor complaining that the tremor has been increasing in recent months, getting worse especially when he feels tense and anxious. Which of the following drugs would be most appropriate to treat the patient’s disorder?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prazosin"},{"label":"B","text":"Clonidine"},{"label":"C","text":"Albuterol"},{"label":"D","text":"Physostigmine"},{"label":"E","text":"Propranolol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b4edaed9b2dc","stem":"A 32-year-old male was brought to the emergency department after the police found him shouting and acting crazy in the street. The man admitted to indulging in cocaine from a new dealer for the past four hours. He was agitated, anxious and exhibits psychotic behavior. Vital signs were: blood pressure 180/100 mm Hg, heart rate 160 bpm, respiratory rate 20/min. Which of the following pairs of drugs were most likely included in the treatment of this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prazosin and propranolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Phenoxybenzamine and labetalol"},{"label":"C","text":"Metyrosine and neostigmine"},{"label":"D","text":"Bethanechol and phenylephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Clonidine and albuterol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0993e94413d7","stem":"A 51-year-old woman presents with a one month history of palpitations, insomnia, nervousness, fatigue, diarrhea and heat intolerance. The physical examination reveals hyperreflexia, lid lag and mild tremor. Vital signs are: blood pressure 160/65 mm Hg and heart rate 95 bpm. Which of the following drugs should be included in an appropriate therapeutic management of this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Carvedilol"},{"label":"C","text":"Prazosin"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenoxybenzamine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b16eee7fe8af","stem":"A new autonomic drug was tested on healthy volunteers. Subjects were treated with saline or with the drug and then the heart rate was measured under resting or exercise conditions. The results were the following: Heart rate (bpm) Saline Drug Resting 70 66 Exercise 150 80 The tested drug most likely a drug from which of the following classes of autonomic drugs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta-blocker"},{"label":"B","text":"Cholinergic agonist"},{"label":"C","text":"cholinergic antagonist"},{"label":"D","text":"Ganglionic blocker"},{"label":"E","text":"Alpha-1 blocker"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d670de92e345","stem":"F G Norepinephrine mainly activates alpha-1, alpha-2, and beta-1 receptors, albuterol mainly activates beta-2 receptors and phenylephrine mainly activates alpha-1 receptors.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epinephrine actually activates all alpha and beta receptors, but has no affinity for D1 receptors."},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol activates beta receptors only."},{"label":"D","text":"Dobutamine activates mainly beta-1 receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Clonidine activates alpha receptors only (mainly alpha-2)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b2cf8e74ab72","stem":"Epinephrine effects on the heart are due to the activation of beta-1 and beta-2 receptors. The postreceptor mechanism common to all beta receptor activation is the stimulation of adenylyl cyclase which in turn increases the amount of cAMP in target tissues.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"This postreceptor mechanism is triggered by the activation of M2 receptors."},{"label":"B","text":"This postreceptor mechanism is triggered by the activation of nicotinic receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"This postreceptor mechanism is triggered by the activation alpha-1 and M3 receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"This postreceptor mechanism is triggered by the activation of alpha-1 receptors"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-10a5b1e73925","stem":"Norepinephrine causes an increase in systolic and diastolic blood pressure. The mean blood pressure is therefore increased and this causes a stimulation of aortic baroreceptors which activates the baroreceptor reflex. The increased vagal discharge overcomes the direct, tachycardic effect of norepinephrine on the heart rate so the final effects is a decrease in heart rate.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of alpha-2 receptors decreases the release of norepinephrine from adrenergic terminal, but this has nothing to do with the actions of norepinephrine given by IV infusion. C, D) Norepinephrine does not activate Nm receptors and does not block muscarinic receptors."},{"label":"E","text":"Chemoreceptors of aortic bodies are stimulated by a decrease, not an increase, in the mean blood pressure."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4c4db98a4ce2","stem":"D Epinephrine causes a alpha-mediated vasoconstriction that increases venous return. This in turn causes a redistribution of blood from the systemic to the pulmonary circulation. In addition the drug constricts arterial and venous vessels. These two actions explain the increased arterial and venous pulmonary pressure. Epinephrine consistently decreases renal blood flow. In the renal vessels alpha receptors predominate and vasoconstriction occurs. This causes a decrease in renal perfusion pressure (in spite of the increased systemic blood pressure) and an increase in vascular resistance. Therefore flow is reduced (flow = perfusion pressure/resistance).","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epinephrine actually increases AV conduction, owing to the activation of cardiac beta receptors (mainly beta-1). B, Alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction is predominant in skin vascular bed. therefore skin blood flow is reduced, not increased."},{"label":"E","text":"Beta-2 mediated vasodilation is predominant in skeletal muscle and therefore the blood flow is increased, not decreased."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6f51335be203","stem":"Phenylephrine is an alpha-1 selective receptor agonist. When given locally in the conjunctival sac it contracts the radial muscle of iris so causing mydriasis. The most common consequence of mydriasis is photophobia since the pupil cannot constrict in response to bright light.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"In most exocrine glands of the body (including lacrimal glands) activation of alpha-1 receptors causes the formation of a concentrated secretion that contains enzymes and mucus. Moreover the alpha receptor mediated vasoconstriction decreases the blood supply to the gland so the actual amount of the lacrimal fluid is in fact decreased, not increased."},{"label":"B","text":"By increasing the blood pressure (both systolic and diastolic) phenylephrine usually triggers a baroreceptor reflex that causes bradycardia, not tachycardia."},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-1 receptor activation has no effect on the ciliary muscle and therefore does not influence eye accommodation."},{"label":"E","text":"(see explanation A)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1d4fd32947b5","stem":"Isoproterenol is a nonselective beta agonist, that is it activates all beta receptors. Activation of beta-1 receptors in the juxtaglomerular cells of macula densa stimulates renin secretion.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Activation of beta-2 receptors in the pancreas actually increases insulin release."},{"label":"C","text":"Activation of beta-2 receptors in the vessels causes vasodilation. The decrease in blood pressure triggers reflex tachycardia, not bradycardia."},{"label":"D","text":"Activation of beta-2 receptors on the surface of mast cells and basophils actually inhibits degranulation of those cells."},{"label":"E","text":"This effect is mediated by the activation of alpha-2 receptors."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05f07f1327a5","stem":"Tremor is a common adverse effect associated with the use of beta-2 agonists such as albuterol. This effect is due to the effects of the drug on Ca++ in skeletal muscle.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta-2 receptor activation increases AV conduction, so AV block is quite unlikely."},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-2 receptor activation actually causes insomnia."},{"label":"D","text":"Hypotension can rarely occur with beta-2 agonists, but postural hypotension is unlikely since the baroreceptor reflex can function normally."},{"label":"E","text":"Arrhythmias can occur with beta-2 agonists but ventricular tachycardia is exceedingly rare."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d7656df4ab9e","stem":"Answers: B D Flushing can occur with beta-2 selective agonists, since activation of beta-2 receptors causes vasodilation. Activation of beta-2 receptors in the liver increases gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis so leading to hyperglycemia.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"By causing peripheral vasodilation beta-2 selective agonist can trigger a reflex tachycardia, but this is due to inhibition, not stimulation of the nucleus ambiguus and of the motor nucleus of vagus."},{"label":"C","text":"The tremor induced by beta-2 selective agonists is not centrally mediated. Instead it is likely because the beta-2 receptor activation accelerates the sequestration of cytosolic Ca++ into sarcoplasmic reticulum, and enhances the discharge of muscle spindles"},{"label":"E","text":"Beta-2 selective agonists do not affect K+ elimination by the kidney. Rather hypokalemia is likely due to the increased K+ uptake by skeletal muscle."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-73ad24eae56a","stem":"Phenylephrine is a selective alpha-1 agonist that causes vasoconstriction in all vessels. This can relieve the nasal congestion, which is due to vasodilation in the nasal mucosa.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ephedrine is a mixed acting sympathomimetic drug that can center the brain and can have actions similar to those of amphetamine. Therefore insomnia is a contraindication, not an indication, for the use of this drug."},{"label":"B","text":"Dobutamine is a drug of choice in cardiogenic shock, but epinephrine remains the most appropriate drug for the anaphylactic shock."},{"label":"C","text":"There is no rationale for the use of norepinephrine in neurogenic migraine."},{"label":"E","text":"Albuterol is actually contraindicated, not indicated, in exertional angina since it can cause tachycardia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dcf6d9b75b0b","stem":"The signs and symptoms strongly suggest that this boy had an acute anaphylactic reaction to the wasp venom. Epinephrine is the drug of choice for the pharmacologic management of anaphylaxis for the following reasons","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its action is almost immediate. Other drugs used for the same purpose (antihistamines, corticosteroids) have a slower onset of action."},{"label":"B","text":"The alpha adrenergic effects maintain blood pressure to overcome the hypotensive effects of histamine and the other mediators of anaphylaxis. These effects also prevent the edema of the glottis that can threaten the patency of the airways and lead to death in few minutes."},{"label":"C","text":"The beta adrenergic effects promote bronchodilation, increase cardiac contractility and inhibit further release of mediator from basophils and mast cells. A, B, D, E) These drugs lack some of epinephrine effects and therefore are not used for the pharmacologic management of anaphylaxis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7e105aa902f9","stem":"The history of the patient suggest that the syncope is most likely due to inadequate cerebral perfusion. There is a long history of successful clinical use of alpha-1 agonists in case of chronic postural hypotension. The therapeutic effectiveness of these drugs is due to the vasoconstriction, which increases both the systolic and diastolic blood pressure, so improving cerebral blood flow.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebral blood flow mainly depends on autoregulation, which can maintain a normal blood flow at arterial pressure of 60 -140 mm Hg. Therefore, in spite of the strong sympathetic innervation of cranial vessels, it has long being stated that sympathetic activity plays little role in controlling cerebral blood flow. In any case an alpha agonist would constrict, not dilate, cerebral vessels."},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha-1 agonists actually decrease the heart rate since the increase in systolic and diastolic blood pressure activates the baroreceptor reflex."},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac output is usually little affected by Alpha-1 agonists. By decreasing the heart rate they would decrease cardiac output, but they would increase it by increasing, venous return, which in turn increases preload. These opposite actions cancel each other, in most cases."},{"label":"E","text":"Beta-1 agonists, not alpha-1 agonists, activate the renin-angiotensin system"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-226329f75f2a","stem":"The patient most likely has Horner’s syndrome, a condition that results from interruption of the sympathetic nerves to the face. The lesion causes vasodilation (loss of the alpha-1 receptor-mediated vascular tone), ptosis (loss of the alpha-1 receptor-mediated contraction of the superior tarsal muscle) miosis (loss of the alpha-1 receptor-mediated contraction of the radial muscle of the iris) and anhydrosis (loss of sympathetic cholinergic firing to the sweat glands). The use of adrenergic drugs can help to localize the lesion. In the present case, the lesion is postganglionic since cocaine (which act by inhibiting the reuptake of catecholamines) does not dilate the constricted pupil because catecholamines have been lost from the nerve endings in the iris (in most cases a lesion of the nerve will cause degeneration of the distal portion of that nerve). In contrast the pupil will dilate in response to epinephrine which directly activates the alpha-1 receptors of the iris.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Face","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"A lesion of the preganglionic sympathetic nerve would have caused the same clinical picture but cocaine would have been effective since the postganglionic adrenergic neurons were intact. C, D) A lesion of either pre or postganglionic parasympathetic nerves would have cause a completely different clinical picture (mydriasis, no ptosis, etc.)"},{"label":"E","text":"Lesions of the sympathetic pathways in the medulla oblongata would cause symptoms similar to those due to lesions of the preganglionic sympathetic nerves."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2271b3a7ba4e","stem":"E All, but one, autonomic adrenoreceptor antagonist reversibly competes with agonists for binding to receptors. Phenoxybenzamine binds to the receptor in an irreversible fashion, i.e noncompetitive. Propranolol is a non-selective beta-blocker, that is can block both beta-1 and beta-2 receptors.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metoprolol is a reversible, not irreversible, beta-blocker."},{"label":"B","text":"Prazosin is an alpha-1, not an alpha-2, selective alpha blocker."},{"label":"C","text":"Labetalol is a reversible, not irreversible, beta-blocker."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2773f860d2e5","stem":"Phenoxybenzamine is a nonselective alpha adrenergic blocker. The blockade of alpha-1 receptors causes peripheral vasodilation which in turn decreases the blood pressure. A reduction in blood pressure decreases the firing rate of carotid baroreceptors. Since the baroreceptor discharge excites the vagal outflow to the heart, a decrease firing rate will have the opposite effect, that is, it would increase the sympathetic outflow. In addition, by blocking presynaptic alpha-2 receptors on adrenergic terminals phenoxybenzamine will increase norepinephrine release, which will accentuate the drug induced tachycardia. B, C) Coronary vasodilation is the consequence, not the cause of the increase in heart rate.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"D","text":"By blocking presynaptic alpha-2 receptors on cholinergic terminals phenoxybenzamine would increase, not decrease, acetylcholine release."},{"label":"E","text":"Phenoxybenzamine has no effect on muscarinic receptors of the heart."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c8d66e7fb61b","stem":"E Epinephrine activates alpha-1 receptors so causing vasoconstriction and beta-2 receptors, so causing vasodilation. Alpha-1 receptor mediated vasoconstriction usually predominates and therefore blood pressure raises. By blocking alpha-1 receptors the beta-2 mediated vasodilating activity of epinephrine is unopposed and blood pressure falls. Activation of alpha-1 receptors contracts the radial muscle of iris, so causing mydriasis. By blocking alpha-1 receptors relaxation occurs.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"By blocking alpha-1 receptors, skin vessels are relaxed and blood flow through the skin is usually increased, not decreased."},{"label":"D","text":"Blockade of alpha-1 receptors causes no direct effects upon the heart. However the vasodilation may cause reflex tachycardia. Therefore heart rate is either minimally affected or increased."},{"label":"D","text":"Alpha-1 receptors are located on smooth muscle of the trigone and bladder internal sphincter. By blocking these receptors relaxation, not contraction, of these muscles occurs."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0fb4a85f9f7","stem":"Prazosin is a selective alpha-1 blocker and therefore will most effectively counteract the effect of a selective alpha-1 agonist like phenylephrine.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoproterenol induces tachycardia by activating beta-1 and (to a lesser extent) beta-2 receptors. The blockade of alpha-1 receptors cannot antagonize this effect."},{"label":"B","text":"Nicotine induces sweating by activating Nn receptors in ganglia which in turn increases sympathetic firing. However the sympathetic fibers innervating sweat glands are cholinergic and therefore, the blockade of alpha-1 receptors cannot antagonize this effect."},{"label":"C","text":"Dopamine increases renal blood flow by activating D1 receptors. By blocking alpha-1 receptors prazosin can also increases renal blood flow."},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine contracts the ciliary muscle by activating M3 receptors. The blockade of alpha-1 receptors cannot antagonize this effect."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cdb85af82eea","stem":"Isoproterenol-induced hyperglycemia is mainly due to beta-2 receptor mediated gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, and therefore it can be effectively counteracted by propranolol, which block beta-2 receptors. All the other listed actions are not antagonized by propranolol.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Nicotine-induced activation of Nn receptor in ganglia increases the firing of postganglionic sympathetic nerves, which in turn cause skin vasoconstriction by activating alpha-1 receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Norepinephrine-induced reflex bradycardia is mediated by the activation of M2 cardiac receptors."},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine-induced mydriasis is mediated by the activation of alpha-1 receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Pilocarpine-induced contraction of bronchial musculature is mediated by the activation of M3 receptors."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1d5084deeafe","stem":"D Metoprolol is a selective beta-blocker. All beta-blocker are effectively used in exertional angina because they decrease cardiac contractility and rate. However they are not used in vasospastic angina, because they do not cause vasodilation (they can be even dangerous since they reduce cardiac output). Beta-blockers are approved by FDA for the prophylactic therapy of migraine. The mechanism of this action is unknown.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Esmolol is a selective beta-blocker with an half-life of about 10 minutes. It is administered by IV infusion to treat cardiovascular disorder (hypertension, arrhythmias, etc.) in acutely ill patients."},{"label":"C","text":"Timolol is use in the treatment of glaucoma; beta-blockers can increase LDL."},{"label":"E","text":"Carvediolol can cause postural hypotension and therefore it is actually contraindicated hypovolemic states, which are the most common cause of postural hypotension."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c6ff8d98a72b","stem":"Selective alpha-1 blockers (like prazosin, terazosin, tamsuolosin etc.) are the drugs of choice for the treatment of impaired bladder emptying in prostatic hyperplasia. By blocking alpha-1 receptors these drugs relax the prostate capsule and the internal sphincter of the bladder (which have plentiful alpha-1 receptors), so allowing a better urine flow. A, B) Beta receptors are not significantly involved in the regulation of the internal sphincter of the bladder.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"Even if activation of alpha-2 receptors would cause a decrease of norepinephrine release, the action is too weak to be clinically significant."},{"label":"E","text":"Activation of M3 receptors would cause a contraction of the detrusor muscle of the bladder, which would increase the urgency to void without improving voiding because of the urethral compression due to prostate hyperplasia."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6ac2dd336e1a","stem":"NE is the neurotransmitter at which Site(s)?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b5ea2ca5b8a","stem":"Acitvation of nicotinic receptors induces release of Epi and NE from which Site(s)?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1ebd86bb2cf2","stem":"α- and β-receptors mediate action of neurotransmitter released at which Site(s)?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a448991e7c6","stem":"Block of nicotinic receptors at which Site(s) will inhibit release of NE from posganglionic neurons?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9d590a8b63d","stem":"Block of appropriate receptors at which Site can result in a marked reduction of blood pressure?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bea3883142b2","stem":"Treatment with this drug would have what effect on NE/Epi content in adrenergic nerve endings/adrenal medulla?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-954340bd1f04","stem":"What effects would this cause?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1a959a7af5be","stem":"How would pretreatment with a MAO inhibitor be expected to affect the response?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-66862f24c7f7","stem":"What are the cardiovascular effects of acute amphetamine administration?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-71aa29aad75b","stem":"Describe the mechanism?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-273ed9a20fb0","stem":"Tranycypromine is an example of a drug in which class?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3806664c199b","stem":"What are the pharmacokinetic differences between catecholamines and non-catecholamines?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-207abf6bbf01","stem":"List at least three drugs that are catecholamines. a) b) c)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b734e1a0d79","stem":"What are the common adverse effects of most catecholamines?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ffffdac091a","stem":"Describe the major differences between the mechanisms and effects of NE and Epi.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-104f0bc670ba","stem":"How does Epi induce marked hyperglycemia?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9093b21198c","stem":"Blockade of α1- and α2-receptors is associated with what effects on blood pressure and heart rate?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5aeec6058ed7","stem":"β−blockers decreased blood pressure by what two prominent mechanisms? a) b)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f239661955cc","stem":"Why are cardioselective β−blockers preferred to non-cardioselective in patients with insulin-dependent (type 1) diabetes mellitus?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"Diabet","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f9acd4e50f2c","stem":"What effect on adrenergic neurotransmission would you expect if a drug inhibited the process at site 2?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bd8eda9fb38a","stem":"What effect does activation of the receptors at site 6 have on adrenergic neurotransmission?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3d5c99d4eea1","stem":"A selective antagonist at Site 8 is: 10 What effect does an inhibitor at Site 5 have on an agent that acts to release NE at Site 4?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e16f759ee2c9","stem":"What is the major adverse effect of a drug that acts as an agonist at Site 7?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-30b29b657151","stem":"What are the effects of a drug that blocks the process at Site 3?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmaco