tructural proteins of the mature virion core?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antiviral","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foscarnet"},{"label":"B","text":"Acyclovir"},{"label":"C","text":"Ribavirin"},{"label":"D","text":"Amantadine"},{"label":"E","text":"Zidovudine 10"},{"label":"A","text":"Efavirenz"},{"label":"B","text":"Zidovudine"},{"label":"C","text":"Maraviroc"},{"label":"D","text":"Lopinavir"},{"label":"E","text":"Raltegravir \\*504b030414000600080000002100828abc13fa0000001c020000130000005b436f6e74656e745f54797065735d2e786d6cac91cb6ac3301045f785fe83d0b6d8 72ba28a5d8cea249777d2cd20f18e4b12d6a8f843409c9df77ecb850ba082d74231062ce997b55ae8fe3a00e1893f354e9555e6885647de3a8abf4fbee29bbd7 2a3150038327acf409935ed7d757e5ee14302999a654e99e393c18936c8f23a4dc072479697d1c81e51a3b13c07e4087e6b628ee8cf5c4489cf1c4d075f92a0b 44d7a07a83c82f308ac7b0a0f0fbf90c2480980b58abc733615aa2d210c2e02cb04430076a7ee833dfb6ce62e3ed7e14693e8317d8cd0433bf5c60f53fea2fe7 065bd80facb647e9e25c7fc421fd2ddb526b2e9373fed4bb902e182e97b7b461e6bfad3f010000ffff0300504b030414000600080000002100a5d6a7e7c00000"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"056b4145d2c92b183a107618d82dcce80edd982f"},{"id":"q-175640ce02e4","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags €PlaceType"},{"label":"C","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags €PlaceName"},{"label":"D","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"E","text":"€place"},{"label":"F","text":"x?$"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-c6af2b89ede8","stem":"Which of the following molecular actions is catalyzed by the penicillin binding proteins?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The transport of penicillins through the poring channels"},{"label":"B","text":"The transpeptidation reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"The breakdown of the beta-lactam ring"},{"label":"D","text":"The inhibition of murein hydrolases"},{"label":"E","text":"Thebinding of penicillins to peptidoglycan layer"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"97f76057cf5e600c268fe997b3687cf7a4d59b53"},{"id":"q-a4bbdc3b1e93","stem":"Which of the following are mechanisms of resistance to beta-lactam antibiotics? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of drug binding to 50S subunit B) Production of enzymes that inactivate the drug"},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease affinity of penicillin binding protein for the drug"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of an efflux pump which expels the drug from bacteria"},{"label":"E","text":"Increased production of autolytic enzymes"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6c92f0959eee2f2b1e1057d4d135b9215e60789d"},{"id":"q-3852d9027589","stem":"Which of the following penicillins are effective against infections causedby MS staphylococci? (Check all that apply)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ticarcillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"label":"C","text":"Cloxacillin"},{"label":"D","text":"Nafcillin"},{"label":"E","text":"Piperacillin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"40f8638a945c331f8e84eda36d18fd2849635cc1"},{"id":"q-79f3f62158a7","stem":"Which of the following statements best explains why broad-spectrum penicillins can cause superinfections?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They lower the immunological defense of the host"},{"label":"B","text":"They select viral mutants that are resistant to most antimicrobial drugs"},{"label":"C","text":"They mask the symptoms of the disease that therefore progresses undetected"},{"label":"D","text":"They alter the normal microflora o f the host"},{"label":"E","text":"They block the activity of mediators essential for a normal inflammatory reaction"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"84b66324a9c7d9e09cce2449cf13629f4d85cd49"},{"id":"q-a2a6d0537530","stem":"A 69-year-old woman from a nursing home was admitted to the hospital for watery diarrhea and hypotension. Salmonella enteritis was isolated from a stool sample and the patient stabilized with rehydration an IV antibiotic. Four days later she deteriorated with abdominal distension and absent bowel sounds. Colonoscopy showed diffuse ulcerations and exudative plaques lining the colonic mucosa. Which of the following antibiotics most likely caused the disease of the patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gentamicin"},{"label":"B","text":"Vancomycin"},{"label":"C","text":"Rifampin"},{"label":"D","text":"Oxacillin E) Ampicillin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"caa102292cbe9a5855e6ae7a8fd5157b283f24e6"},{"id":"q-4955fae21a8d","stem":"A 7-year-old girl presented with fever and a productive cough of thick, greenish sputum. Her medical history was significant for cystic fibrosis for the past 3 years and several episodes of pneumonia. The Gram stain of the sputum revealed gram-negative bacilli. Growth of the organism on agar culture exhibited a blue-green pigment. Which of the following beta-lactam drugs would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piper acillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Cefazolin"},{"label":"C","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"label":"D","text":"Clavulanate"},{"label":"E","text":"Oxacillin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"83f1a49ec510fd211dda0466d5c024c75e8e3b16"},{"id":"q-500ff23db628","stem":"A 10-year-old boy presents with fever of 102 F and complains of lower back, knee and wrist pain. He has a history of a severe sore throat 3 weeks earlier. Physical examination reveals subcutaneous nodules over the elbowsand wrists and a serpiginous, flat, painless rash on the anterior trunk. An erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 120/h and the antistreptolysin-O titer is elevated. Which of the following treatments would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penicillin G and aspirin"},{"label":"B","text":"Amoxicillin and erythromycin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftriaxone and acetaminophen"},{"label":"D","text":"Tobramycin and aspirin"},{"label":"E","text":"Penicillin G and acetaminophen"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d1303a3e8d5660008228dde3e34f6953b9b76c35"},{"id":"q-a723f296d60f","stem":"A 5-year-old boy presented with fever and sharp pain at his left ear. On physical examination the left tympanic membrane was red, opaque and bulging. Amoxicillin was prescribed but three days later the symptoms were not reduced. The pediatrician decided to change the therapy and prescribed amoxicillin/clavulanate. Which of the following statements best explains the advantage of adding clavulanate to amoxicillin?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The drug inhibits the inactivation of amoxicillin by beta-lactamase enzymes"},{"label":"B","text":"The drug is active against Pseudomonas and Serratia species"},{"label":"C","text":"The drug increases amoxicillin plasma concentration by inhibiting renal secretion"},{"label":"D","text":"The drug increases amoxicillin diffusion across the outer bacterial membrane"},{"label":"E","text":"The drug inhibits the synthesis of peptidoglycan \\*504b030414000600080000002100e9de0fbfff0000001c020000130000005b436f6e74656e745f54797065735d2e786d6cac91cb4ec3301045f748fc83e52d4a 9cb2400825e982c78ec7a27cc0c8992416c9d8b2a755fbf74cd25442a820166c2cd933f79e3be372bd1f07b5c3989ca74aaff2422b24eb1b475da5df374fd9ad 5689811a183c61a50f98f4babebc2837878049899a52a57be670674cb23d8e90721f90a4d2fa3802cb35762680fd800ecd7551dc18eb899138e3c943d7e503b6 b01d583deee5f99824e290b4ba3f364eac4a430883b3c092d4eca8f946c916422ecab927f52ea42b89a1cd59c254f919b0e85e6535d135a8de20f20b8c12c3b0 0c895fcf6720192de6bf3b9e89ecdbd6596cbcdd8eb28e7c365ecc4ec1ff1460f53fe813d3cc7f5b7f020000ffff0300504b030414000600080000002100a5d6 a7e7c0000000360100000b0000005f72656c732f2e72656c73848fcf6ac3300c87ef85bd83d17d51d2c31825762fa590432fa37d00e1287f68221bdb1bebdb4f c7060abb0884a4eff7a93dfeae8bf9e194e720169aaa06c3e2433fcb68e1763dbf7f82c985a4a725085b787086a37bdbb55fbc50d1a33ccd311ba548b6309512"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b37a419f2a4025866fbc012befa289cc7ad2a241"},{"id":"q-9e06356f70c3","stem":"Discuss the case together and answer the related questions in the time you are given Questions","type":"Essay / Structured Essay","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-181378c91e2b","stem":"What is the potency relationship between drug A and drug B?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A is more potent"},{"label":"B","text":"B is more potent"},{"label":"C","text":"They are of equal potency"},{"label":"D","text":"A conclusion cannot be drawn"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b77b35ec861","stem":"What is the potency relationship between drug B and drug C?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"B is more potent"},{"label":"B","text":"C is more potent"},{"label":"C","text":"They are of equal potency"},{"label":"D","text":"A conclusion cannot be drawn"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-14bbde429a3e","stem":"What is the efficacy relationship between drug A and drug C? • What is selective toxicity? • What is therapeutic index? • List the mechanisms involved in acquiring bacterial resistance? ampicillin, which is excreted by glomerular filtration and tubular secretion? • What is the mechanism of action of β -lactam antibiotics? • What is the mode of bacterial resistance of penicillins? • How are sulphonamides classified? • What is the mechanism of action of sulphonamides? • What is the mechanism of action of trimethoprim? • What are the pharmacokinetic properties of sulphonamides? • What are the major therapeutic indications of sulphonamides? • What are the modes of action of protein synthesis inhibitors? • What are the clinical uses of macrolides?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A is more efficacious"},{"label":"B","text":"C is more efficacious"},{"label":"C","text":"They are of equal efficacy"},{"label":"D","text":"A conclusion cannot be drawn CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 2: Basic Principles of Pharmacology - Part 2 9 CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 2: Basic Principles of Pharmacology - Part 2 10 Session 3: Principles of Antimicrobial Action Learning Objectives By the end of this session, students are expected to be able to: • Define common terms in relation to antimicrobial agents • Describe bacterial resistance • Explain different mechanisms involved in acquiring bacterial resistance • Explain the classification of antimicrobial drugs based upon the mechanism of action Definition of Key Terms in Relation to Antimicrobial Agents • Antibiotics: Substances produced by some microorganisms (or by pharmaceutical chemists) that kills or inhibits the growth of other microorganisms. • Bacteriostatic antibiotic: An antimicrobial drug that inhibits microbial growth but requires host defence mechanisms to eradicate the infection. • It does not kill bacteria. o For example chloramphenicol, tetracycline and erythromycin. o They stop bacterial growth and allow the host’s immune factors to ultimately clear the infection. • Bacteriocidal antibiotic: An antimicrobial drug that can eradicate an infection in the absence of host defence mechanisms. • It kills bacteria. o For example penicillin, cephalosporin, and most aminoglycosides cause microbial death by lysis. • They rely less on host immunity for clearing of bacterial infections. • Synergistic effect: The summing of the simultaneous effects of two or more drugs such that the combined effect is greater than the effect of either of the drugs when they are given alone. o When two bactericidal antibiotics are used in combination, one of the two drugs must show at least four-fold increase in antibacterial activities for a synergism to exist between the two drugs for example penicillin + streptomycin. • Additive Effect: When two antibiotics agents with the same mechanisms of action are used. • Antagonism: The effect of two or more drugs such that the combined effect is less than the sum of the effects produced by each agent separately o Bacteriostatic antibiotics are antagonistic to bactericidal agents. o For example, chloramphenicol has been shown to antagonize the bactericidal activities of penicillin in the treatment of Pneumococcal meningitis. • Selective Toxicity: The ability of a drug to be more toxic to the invader than to the host. o This is a useful property of antimicrobial drugs. • Therapeutic Index: A measure of drug safety obtained by comparing the amount of the drug that produces an effective response to the amount that produces a toxic response. o It is calculated by dividing the lethal dose by the minimum effective dose. Therapeutic index= Lethal Dose LD50 Effective Dose ED50 o A large number indicates a wide margin of safety o A small number indicates a small margin of safety CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 3: Principles of Antimicrobial Action 11 • A drug with a higher therapeutic index is safer than one with a lower therapeutic index. Resistance to Antibacterial Drugs Antimicrobial Resistance • This is the capacity of bacteria to survive exposure to a defined concentration of an antimicrobial substance. o Bacteria are said to be resistant if their growth is not halted by the maximum level of an antibiotic that is tolerated by the host. o Many organisms have adapted, through spontaneous mutation or acquired resistance and selection, and developed more virulent strains. o Many of these mutations/adaptations are resistant to multiple antibiotics. • Antibiotic resistance in bacteria spreads in three ways: o Transfer of bacteria between people  Resistance in bacterial populations can be spread from person to person by bacteria, from bacterium to bacterium by plasmids, from plasmid to plasmid (or chromosome) by transposons. o Transfer of resistance genes between bacteria (usually on plasmids)  Plasmids are extra chromosomal genetic elements that can replicate independently and can carry genes coding for resistance to antibiotics.  The main method of transfer of genes from one bacterium to another is by plasmids. The bacterium forms a connecting tube with other bacteria through which the plasmids pass. o Transfer of resistance genes between genetic elements within bacteria (on transposons)  Transposons are stretches of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) that can be transposed from one plasmid to another, from a plasmid to a chromosome or vice versa. Mechanisms of Resistance to Antibiotics • Bacteria have evolved numerous strategies for resisting the action of antibiotics and antibacterial agents. • This is particularly true of those bacteria that are antibiotic producers. • Bacteria that produce antibiotics do so to gain a selective advantage over other, competing microbes in their natural environment. • If they were sensitive to their own metabolic products, such a selective advantage would be lost. • Bacteria may display antibiotic resistance by one or more of the following mechanisms:- o They may lack a target for the antibiotic  Chlamydia does not have peptidoglycan and are not susceptible to the action of penicillins. o The antibiotic target may be inaccessible  Peptidoglycan in gram-negative bacteria is inaccessible to penicillins that cannot penetrate the gram-negative outer membrane.  Efflux pumps can actively pump out antibiotics from cells.  Gram-negative bacteria resist the activity of tetracyclines by this important mechanism. o The antibiotic target may be modified to prevent the action of the drug  Trimethoprim resistance is manifest by alterations in the enzyme target CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 3: Principles of Antimicrobial Action 12  Quinolone resistance is affected by point mutations in the DNA gyrase, which prevent binding of the drug to its target. o The antibiotic may be chemically modified or destroyed  Important examples include the huge range of b-lactamases and the various aminoglycosides-modifying enzymes.  The b-lactam bond can be hydrolyzed by a large family of enzymes known as the"},{"label":"B","text":"lactamases. Some b-lactamases have a preferential activity against penicillins and these are referred to as penicillinases. Cephalosporinases are more active against cephalosporins.  Chloramphenicol resistance is most often manifest by acetylation by the chloramphenicol acetyl transferase enzyme. o Bacteria may elaborate alternative pathways, avoiding the drug target;  Staphylococcus aureus results from the production of an additional penicillin binding protein 2, which is not susceptible to inhibition by penicillins. Classification of Antimicrobial Drugs • Antimicrobial drugs can be classified in a number of ways, according to: o Their chemical structure  β-Lactams, aminoglycosides o Mechanism of action  Cell wall synthesis inhibitors, or activity against o Types of organisms  Bacteria, fungi, viruses Classification of Antimicrobial Drugs by Mechanism of Action • Inhibitors of cell wall synthesis o β-lactam antibiotics for example Penicillin, Cephalosporins and Cephamycins • Inhibitors of protein synthesis o Aminoglycosides, Tetracycline, Macrolides and Chloramphenicol • Inhibitors of nucleic acid synthesis o Antimicrobial agents who interfere with the synthesis or action of folate for example Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim • Antimicrobial agents affecting topoisomerase o Quinolones Refer to Handout 3.1: Classification of Antibiotics According to their Chemical Structure. Key Points • Bacteriostatic antibiotic inhibits microbial growth and Bactericidal antibiotic – kills bacterial • Resistance to antibiotics occurs due to the following: o They may lack a target for the antibiotic o The antibiotic target may be inaccessible o The antibiotic target may be modified to prevent the action of the drug o The antibiotic may be chemically modified or destroyed o Bacteria may elaborate alternative pathways, avoiding the drug target; • Antimicrobial drugs can be classified according to: o Their chemical structure CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 3: Principles of Antimicrobial Action 13 o Mechanism of action o Types of organisms Evaluation References • Hardman, J.G. et al. (2001): Goodman and Gilman’s: The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics (10th ed.). New York: McGraw Hill. • Laurence, D. R. et al. (1997): Clinical Pharmacology (8th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. • Rang, H. & Dale, M. (2007). Pharmacology. (6th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 3: Principles of Antimicrobial Action 14 Handout 3.1: Classification of Antibiotics According to their Chemical Structure Class (chemical structure) Mechanism of action Examples"},{"label":"B","text":"lactam antibiotics Inhibit bacterial cell wall Penicillins • Penicillins synthesis • Penicillin G • Cephalosporins • Amoxicillin • Carbapenems • Flucloxacillin Cephalosporins • Cefoxitin • Cefotaxime • Ceftriaxone Carbapenem • Imipenem Macrolides Inhibit bacterial protein • Erythromycin synthesis • Azithromycin • Clarithromycin Tetracyclines Inhibit bacterial protein • Tetracycline synthesis • Minocycline • Doxycycline • Lymecycline Fluoroquinolones Inhibit bacterial DNA • Norfloxacin synthesis • Ciprofloxacin • Enoxacin • Ofloxacin Sulphonamides Blocks bacterial cell • Co-trimoxazole metabolism by inhibiting • Trimethoprim enzymes Aminoglycosides Inhibit bacterial protein • Gentamicin synthesis • Amikacin Imidazoles Inhibit bacterial DNA • Metronidazole synthesis Peptides Inhibit bacterial cell wall • Bacitracin synthesis Lincosamides Inhibit bacterial protein • Clindamycin synthesis • Lincomycin Other Inhibit bacterial protein • Fusidic acid synthesis • Mupirocin CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 3: Principles of Antimicrobial Action 15 CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 3: Principles of Antimicrobial Action 16 Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics Learning Objectives By the end of this session, students are expected to be able to: • Explain mechanism of action of β-lactam antibiotics • Describe classification of penicillins • Describe pharmacokinetic properties of penicillins • Explain the mode of bacterial resistance of penicillins Basic Pharmacology of β-lactam Antibiotics • Beta-lactam antibiotics are among the most commonly prescribed drugs, grouped together based upon a shared structural feature, the beta-lactam ring. • Beta-lactam antibiotics include: o Penicillins o Cephalosporins o Cephamycins o Carbapenems o Monobactams o Beta-lactamase inhibitors - clavulanic acid • The original group of beta-lactam antibiotics is the penicillins, whose target is the cell wall • They interfere with cross-linking of the cell wall in actively growing cells • The targets of the penicillins are called penicillin binding proteins (PBPs) and are enzymes involved in synthesis of the cell wall Mechanism of Action of β-lactam Antibiotics • The activity of antibiotics depends on three things: o Affinity for the target (e.g. how well penicillin binds PBPs) o Permeability properties (e.g. through capsule, peptidoglycan) o Stability to bacterial enzymatic degradation (e.g. beta-lactamases) • Interferes with the last step of the bacterial cell wall synthesis peptidoglycan o After attachment to binding sites on the bacterium they inhibit the transpeptidation enzyme that cross-links the peptide chains attached to the backbone of the peptidoglycan o The final bactericidal event is the inactivation of an inhibitor of the autolytic enzymes in the cell wall. This leads to lysis of the bacterium Classification of Penicillins • There are 4 classes of penicillins, based upon their ability to kill various types of bacteria from narrow to broad range of effectiveness. Natural Penicillins • Include - Penicillin G, Procaine, Penicillin G, Penicillin V and Benzathine. • The natural penicillins were the first agents in the penicillin family to be introduced for clinical use. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 17 • They are effective against gram-positive strains of streptococci, staphylococci, and some gram-negative bacteria such as meningococcal. • Penicillin V is the drug of choice for the treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis. It is also useful for anaerobic coverage in patients with oral cavity infections. Penicillinases-Resistant Penicillins • Include - Cloxacillin, Dicloxacillin, Methicillin, Nafcillin and Oxacillin. • Methicillin was the first member of this group and it is rarely used because of its toxicity. • The penicillinases-resistant penicillins have a narrower spectrum of activity than the natural penicillins. • Their antimicrobial efficacy is aimed directly against penicillinases-producing strains of gram-positive cocci, particularly staphylococcal species and these drugs are sometimes called anti-staphylococcal penicillins. Broad-Spectrum Penicillins (Aminopenicillins) • Include - Ampicillin, Amoxicillin, and Bacampicillin). • The aminopenicillins were the first penicillins discovered to be active against gram- negative bacteria (such as E. coli and H. influenza), which do not produce beta-lactamase enzymes. • Aminopenicillins are acid-resistant so are administered orally. • Orally administered amoxicillin and ampicillin are used primarily to treat mild infections such as otitis media, sinusitis, bronchitis, urinary tract infections and bacterial diarrhea. Extended Spectrum Penicillins • Extended Spectrum Penicillins include both alpha-carboxypenicillins (carbenicillin and ticarcillin) and acylaminopenicillins (piperacillin, azlocillin, and mezlocillin). • These agents have similar spectrums of activity as the aminopenicillins but with additional activity against several gram negative organisms of the family Enterobacteriaceae, including many strains of Pseudomonas aeruginosa. • Like the aminopenicillins, these agents are susceptible to inactivation by beta-lactamases. • These agents may be used alone or in combination with Aminoglycosides. Refer to Handout 4.1: Cephalosporin Pharmacokinetic Properties of Penicillin • When given orally, different penicillins are absorbed at different degrees depending on their stability in acid and their adsorption to food. • Penicillins can be given by intramuscular or intravenous injection. • Penicillins become widely distributed in the body fluids, passing into joints, into pleural and pericardial cavities, into bile, saliva, milk and across the placenta. • Being lipid-insoluble, they do not enter mammalian cells. • They do not cross the blood-brain barrier (BBB) unless the meninges are inflamed, in which case they readily reach therapeutically effective concentrations in cerebro-spinal fluid (CSF). • Elimination of most penicillin is mainly renal and occurs rapidly. 90% occurs by tubular secretion. • Tubular secretion of penicillins can be partially blocked by probenecid, which raises their plasma concentration and prolongs their action. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 18 • Penicillins, often combined with other antibiotics, are crucially important in antibacterial chemotherapy. Refer to Handout 4.2: Clinical Uses of Penicillin. Activity: Small Group Discussion Instructions In small groups, you will work to answer the following question: • What effect would the administration of probenecid have on the administration of The instructor will ask a group to volunteer their responses, so be prepared to share your group’s answers with the class. Resistance to Penicillin • The production of β-lactamases: o β -lactamase production is particularly important in staphylococci, though other organisms (Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Haemophilus spp.) also produce these enzymes. o Streptococci do not produce β-lactamase enzymes. o One solution is the concomitant use of β -lactamase inhibitors, such as clavulanic acid. • A reduction in the permeability of the outer membrane and thus a decreased ability of the drug to penetrate to the target site. • The occurrence of modified penicillin-binding sites. This is particularly important in methicillin-resistant staphylococci. Key Points • Penicillins are the major antibiotics that inhibit bacterial cell wall synthesis. • Selective toxicity of the penicillins is due to specific actions on the synthesis of a cellular structure that is unique to the microorganism. • There are 4 classes of penicillins, based upon their ability to kill various types of bacteria from narrow to broad range of effectiveness. These include: o Natural penicillins o Penicillinases resistant penicillins o Broad spectrum penicillins (aminopenicillins) o Extended spectrum penicillins • Mechanisms underlying microbial resistance to cell wall synthesis inhibitors include the production of antibiotic-inactivating enzymes, changes in the structure of target receptors, and decreases in the permeability of microbes’ cellular membranes to antibiotics. Evaluation CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 19 References • Hardman, J.G. et al. (2001): Goodman and Gilman’s: The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics (10th ed.). New York: McGraw Hill. • Laurence, D. R. et al. (1997): Clinical Pharmacology (8th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. • Rang, H. & Dale, M. (2007). Pharmacology. (6th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 20 Handout 4.1: Cephalosporins General Information • The cephalosporin ring structure is derived from 7-aminocephalosporanic acid • While the penicillins are derived from 6-aminopenicillanic acid • Both structures contain the basic beta-lactam ring but the cephalosporin structure allows for more gram-negative activity than the penicillins and aminopenicillins. • Cephalosporins are grouped into ‘generations’ by their antimicrobial properties. • The first cephalosporins were designated ‘first generation.’ while later; more extended spectrum cephalosporins were classified as second and third generation cephalosporins. • Three generations of cephalosporins are recognized and a fourth has been proposed. • Significantly, each newer generation of cephalosporins has greater gram-negative antimicrobial properties than the preceding generation. • Conversely, the ‘older’ generations of cephalosporins have greater gram positive (staphylococcus and streptococcus) coverage than the ‘newer’ generations (except ceftriaxone/cefotaxime). • Gram-positive coverage diminishes as gram-negative coverage improves with each successive generation of cephalosporins. o Because of this relationship, first generation cephalosporins are useful in treating skin and urinary infections. • Total daily dosage of individual cephalosporins is similar within each generation. • Frequency of dosing decreases with increasing generation(s). • Palatability improves (probably) with increasing generations. • Most second and third generation cephalosporins have not been approved for use in patients less than six months of age. • First generation cephalosporins have activity against staphylococcus and streptococcus, along with Proteus, E. coli, and Klebsiella. • The second generation cephalosporins also have H. influenza activity • The third generations have greatly expanded gram negative coverage. First Generation Cephalosporins • Cefadroxil (Duricef-generic available) • Cephalexin (Keflex-generic available) • Cephradine (Velosef-generic available) Second Generation Cephalosporins • Cefaclor (Ceclor) • Cefuroxime Axtel (Ceftin) • Cefprozil (Cefzil) • Loracarbef (Lorabid) Clinical Uses • Active against beta-lactamase producing H. influenza and have been primarily been used in to treat sinusitis otitis or lower respiratory infections. • Can be used in mixed anaerobic infections. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 21 • Cefuroxime is the only second generation drug that crosses the blood-brain barrier (BBB) but is less effective in treatment of meningitis than ceftriaxone or cefotaxime and should not be used. Third Generation Cephalosporins • Ceftazidime • Ceftizoxime • Cefpodoxime • Ceftriaxone Clinical Uses • Have expanded gram negative coverage and the ability of some to cross the blood-brain barrier (BBB) • Used to treat meningitis caused by Pneumococci, meningococci and H. influenza. • Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime are the most active cephalosporins against penicillin-resistant strains of pneumococci. • Should not be used to treat enterobacter infections even if the isolate appears susceptible due to the emergence of resistance. Adverse Effects Allergic Manifestations • The cephalosporins should be avoided or used with caution in individuals allergic to penicillins (about 5 to 7% show reactivity). • In contrast, the incidence of allergic reactions to cephalosporins is 1-2% in patients without a history of allergy to penicillins. Disulfiram-like Effect • When cefoperazone is ingested with alcohol or alcohol-containing medications, a disulfiram-like effect is seen, because these cephalosporins block the second step in alcohol oxidation, which results in the accumulation of acetaldehyde. Bleeding • Bleeding can occur with cefamandole or cefoperazone, because of anti-vitamin K effects. Administration of the vitamin corrects the problem. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 22 Handout 4.2: Clinical Uses of Penicillin Penicillin can be used to treat: • Bacterial meningitis due to N. meningitidis, Strep. pneumoniae: Benzylpenicillin, high doses intravenously • Bone and Joint Infections (e.g. with Staph. aureus): flucloxacillin • Skin and soft tissue infections (e.g. with Strep. Pyogenes or Staph. aureus): benzylpenicillin, flucloxacillin. • Animal bites (often mixed organisms): Co-amoxiclav. • Pharyngitis (from Strep. pyogenes): Phenoxymethylpenicillin orally. • Otitis Media (organisms commonly include Strep.pyogenes, H. influenzae): Amoxicillin • Bronchitis in patients with chronic obstructive airways disease (mixed infections common): Amoxycillin orally. • Community-acquired Pneumonia, not severely ill (e.g. with Strep. pneumoniae): amoxycillin orally. • Urinary Tract Infection (e.g. with E. coli): Amoxycillin orally. • Gonorrhoea: Amoxycillin plus probenecid (orally), plus other antibiotics • Syphilis: Usually, benzathine penicillin and procaine penicillin intramuscularly is spared for neurosyphylis. • Endocarditis (e.g. with Strep. viridans or Enterococcus faecalis): Benzylpenicillin, plus an aminoglycoside. • Serious infections with Pseudomonas aeruginosa: piperacillin intravenously. Unwanted Effects • Hyper-sensitivity reactions. • Skin rashes and fever are common. • Acute anaphylactic shock which may, in some cases, be fatal but is fortunately very rare. • Haematologic disturbances. • Alteration of the bacterial flora in the gut, particularly the broad-spectrum type penicillin. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 23 CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 4: β-Lactam Antibiotics 24 Session 5: Antifolate Drugs (Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim) Learning Objectives By the end of this session, students are expected to be able to: • Classify sulphonamides by giving an example for each group • Discuss the mechanism of action of sulphonamides • Discuss the mechanism of action of trimethoprim • Describe the pharmacokinetic properties of sulphonamides • Discuss the principles for use of combination of sulphonamides • Describe the major therapeutic indications of sulphonamides alone, and in combination with trimethoprim (cotrimoxazole) • Describe the major adverse effects associated with the use of sulphonamides and trimethoprim Classification of Sulphonamides • Short-acting, well absorbed in the gastrointestinal tract such as sulphamethoxazole and Co-trimoxazole (the combination of sulphamethoxazole with trimethoprim). • Long-acting, well absorbed in the gastrointestinal tract such as sulphametopyrazine. • Poorly absorbed in the gastrointestinal tract such as sulphasalazine. This medicine is used for topical application. • Sulphasalazine is used for inflammatory bowel disease. Mechanism of Action of Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim • Folic acid is essential in the bacterial growth and the only source of folic acid to bacteria is by synthesis. • These medicines block the synthesis through the following mechanisms of action. Sulphonamides • The medicine is a synthetic chemical compound of structural analogues of Para-Amino Benzoic Acid (PABA). • They compete with PABA in the folic acid synthesis pathway. • Pathway produces purines and pyrimidines - building blocks of nucleic acids (Nucleic acid synthesis inhibitors). • The medicine does so only to bacteria and not to human cell because humans cannot synthesize folic acid they only receive it through diet. That is why this mechanism is called selective toxicity. • The action of a sulphonamide is to inhibit growth of the bacteria, not to kill them. It is bacteriostatic rather than bactericidal. • The action is negated by the presence of pus and the products of tissue breakdown since these contain thymidine and purines, which bacteria use to bypass the need for folic acid. Trimethoprim • Trimethoprim is a diaminopyrimidine/ pyrimidine analogue. • It inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase by combining with the enzyme Dehydrofolate reductase (DHF). CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 5: Antifolate Drugs (Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim) 25 • The enzyme is present in human cells but trimethoprim binds more to the bacterial enzyme than human cell enzyme. This is what is called selective toxicity based on differential affinity for DHF from different sources (e.g. mammalian versus bacterial). • Trimethoprim-sulphonamide combination has broad range of activity. o Displays synergism when used in combination with sulphonamides (co-trimoxazole). o Remains the treatment of choice for some infections:  Pneumocystis infections in HIV&AIDS or any immunocompromized patients.  Salmonella typhi • Sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydropteroate synthase. • Trimethoprim inhibits dihydrofolate reductase. Figure 1: Mechanism of Action, Inhibitory Effects of Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim on Folic Acid Synthesis Source: Foster, 1996 Pharmacokinetics of Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim Sulphonamides • Sulphonamides are broad spectrum bacteriostatic agents effective against Gram-positive & Gram-negative bacteria. • Most sulphonamides are readily absorbed in the gastrointestinal tract and reach maximum concentrations in the plasma in 4-6 hours. • They are usually not given topically, mainly because of the risk of sensitisation and allergic reactions. An exception is silver sulphadiazine which is used topically in the treatment of infected burns. • The drugs pass into inflammatory exudates, and cross the placental barrier; most reach an effective concentration in the cerebro-spinal fluid (CSF), but they are no longer used to treat most of the central nervous system (CNS) infections except for the trimethoprim which is used to treat CNS toxoplasmosis. • They are metabolised mainly in the liver, the major product being an acetylated derivative which lacks antibacterial action. They are excreted in the urine. • Resistance, due to the synthesis of an enzyme insensitive to the drug. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 5: Antifolate Drugs (Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim) 26 Trimethoprim • Given orally, is fully absorbed in the GIT. • It reaches high concentrations in the lungs and the kidneys and fairly high concentrations in the CSF. • Since trimethoprim is a weak base, its elimination by the kidney increases with decreasing urinary pH. Principles for Use of Combinations of Sulphonamides • Combination of sulphonamides must include two drugs with a synergistic action i.e. One inhibiting dihydropteroate synthase (Sulphamethoxazole) and the other inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase (Trimethoprim). • Inhibition of these two enzymes interferes with two serial steps in folic acid synthesis, and thereby inhibits synthesis of nucleic acids and proteins. Clinical Uses of Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim Absolute indications are very few, but sulphonamides may be used as follows: • Combined with trimethoprim (co-trimoxazole) for Pneumocystis jiroveci (also known as P.carinii). Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia (PCP) almost always occurs when the CD4<200. • Combined with pyrimethamine for drug-resistant malaria and for toxoplasmosis. • In inflammatory bowel disease: sulfasalazine. • For infected burns: silver sulfadiazine given topically and for some sexually transmitted infections (e.g. trachoma, chlamydia, and chancroid). Clinical Uses of Trimethoprim • For urinary tract and respiratory infections: trimethoprim, used on its own, is usually preferred. • For infection with Pneumocystis jiroveci, which causes pneumonia in patients with AIDS co-trimoxazole is used in high dose. Unwanted Effects of Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim Adverse Effects of Sulphonamides • Mild to moderate side-effects are nausea and vomiting, headache, and mental depression. • Crystalluria, due to precipitation of acetylated metabolites in the urine. o It can be prevented by giving plenty of fluids and keeping the urine alkaline, and is less likely to occur with the more water-soluble drugs. • Hypersensitivity reactions • Haemolytic anaemia • Drug interaction: Due to the high affinity to plasma proteins, some of the newer products may free other drugs from their binding proteins, thereby may potentiate activities of the freed drugs. These include anticoagulants, and hypoglycaemic agents of sulfonylurea group. Unwanted Effects of Trimethoprim • Nausea, vomiting, blood disorders and skin rashes. • Folate deficiency, with resultant megaloblastic anaemia a toxic effect related to the pharmacological action of trimethoprim can be prevented by giving folinic acid. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 5: Antifolate Drugs (Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim) 27 Key Points • Sulphonamides are bacteriostatic, they act by interfering with folate synthesis and thus with nucleotide synthesis. • Unwanted effects include crystalluria and hypersensitivities. • Trimethoprim is also bacteriostatic. • It acts by antagonising folate. • Co-trimoxazole is a combination of trimethoprim with sulfamethoxazole, which affects bacterial nucleotide synthesis at two sites in the pathway. Evaluation References • Hardman, J.G. et al. (2001): Goodman and Gilman’s: The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics (10th ed.). New York: McGraw Hill. • Laurence, D. R. et al. (1997): Clinical Pharmacology (8th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. • Rang, H. & Dale, M. (2007). Pharmacology. (6th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 5: Antifolate Drugs (Sulphonamides and Trimethoprim) 28 Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics Learning Objectives By the end of this session, students are expected to be able to: • List protein synthesis inhibitor antibiotics included in the national essential drug list (NEDLIST) • Describe the modes of action of protein synthesis inhibitor antibiotics • Describe the doses and regimen of protein synthesis inhibitor antibiotics, according to standard treatment guideline (STG) and NEDLIST • Identify caution, contraindication, interaction and side effects of the protein synthesis inhibitor antibiotics Antimicrobials Agents Affecting Bacterial Protein Synthesis Antimicrobial agents affecting bacterial protein synthesis can be classified into the following classes: • Antimicrobials that bind to the 30s ribosomal subunit o Aminoglycosides (bactericidal) o Streptomycin, kanamycin, gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin, netilmicin and neomycin (topical) o Tetracyclines (bacteriostatic) o Tetracycline, minocycline and doxycycline o Spectinomycin (bacteriostatic) • Antimicrobials that bind to the 50s ribosomal subunit o Chloramphenicol, lincomycin, clindamycin (bacteriostatic) o Macrolides (bacteriostatic) - Erythromycin (also azithromycin, clarithromycin) • Antimicrobials that interfere with elongation factors o Fusidic acid (bacteriostatic) • Inhibitors of RNA synthesis and function o Rifampin, rifamycin, rifampicin (bactericidal) • Inhibitors of DNA synthesis and function o Quinolones - nalidixic acid, ciprofloxacin, oxolinic acid (bactericidal) Choice of Suitable Medicines • Before selecting an antibacterial the clinician must first consider two factors related to the patient and those related to the causative organism. • Factors related to the patient which must be considered include: o History of allergy o Renal and hepatic function o Susceptibility to infection (i.e. whether immunocompromised) o Ability to tolerate drugs by mouth o Severity of illness o Age o Whether taking other medication o If female, whether pregnant, breast-feeding or taking an oral contraceptive. o Duration of therapy, dosage, and route of administration depend on site, type and severity of infection and response. • Factors related to the causative organism include: CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 29 o Sensitivity of the medicine to the organism o Ability of the organism to form resistant strains to the medicine Refer to: • Handout 6.1: Before Starting Therapy • Standard Treatment Guideline (STG) of Tanzania and National Essential Drug List (NEDLIST) of Tanzania Amino Glycosides • Aminoglycoside are classified into the following families: o Streptomycin family – Streptomycin o Gentamicin family – Gentamicin o Kanamycin family – Amikacin, Tobramycin and Neomycin Indications/Clinical Uses of Amino Glycosides • The primary target is protein synthesis • They also may affect membrane transport mechanisms. • Aminoglycosides have bactericidal activity against most gram-negative bacteria including Enterobacter, E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus, Pseudomanas, Salmonella, and Shigella. • Aminoglycosides are synergistic with antimicrobials (cell wall active agents) most strains of enterococcus are resistant to aminoglycosides alone, however when used in combination with penicillins they are often effective in enterococcal endocarditis due to synergistic antimicrobial mechanisms. • Streptomycin is active against Mycobacterium Tuberculosis and is now almost entirely reserved for tuberculosis • Gentamicin is the aminoglycosides most commonly used.Topically, neomycin is also effective for management of infections of the conjunctiva or external ear • A disadvantage of the aminoglycosides is their association with nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity Aminoglycosides levels are monitored during therapy. Mechanism of Action of Amino Glycosides • Aminoglycosides are bactericidal inhibitors of protein synthesis. • Aminoglycosides bind to the 30S ribosomal subunit and interfere with protein synthesis by changing shape of 30S portion. • Their penetration through the bacterial cell envelope is partly dependent on oxygen- dependent active transport, and they have minimal action against anaerobic organisms • The effect of the aminoglycosides is bactericidal and is enhanced by agents that interfere with cell wall synthesis. Resistance • Inactivation by microbial enzymes. o The microorganism produces a transferase enzymes or enzymes that inactivate the aminoglycoside by adenylation acetylation or phosphorylation o Amikacin was designed as a poor substrate for these enzymes; but some organisms have developed enzymes that inactivate this agent. • Impaired entry into the cell o Resistance due to failure of penetration can be largely overcome by the concomitant use of penicillin and/or vancomycin. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 30 • The receptor protein on the 30S ribosome may be deleted or altered as result of mutation Pharmacokinetic Aspects of Amino Glycosides • Poorly absorbed from gastrointestinal tract (GIT) • Highly polar • All aminoglycosides (except neomycin) must be given parenterally to achieve adequate serum levels • High concentration in renal cortex → nephrotoxicity • High concentration in endolymph & perilymph of inner ear → ototoxicity • Excreted rapidly by glomerular filtration • Distribution: o Levels achieved in most tissues are low, and penetration into most body fluids is variable o Concentrations in cerebro-spinal fluid (CSF) are inadequate even when the meninges are inflamed o All cross the placental barrier and may accumulate in foetal plasma and amniotic fluid Refer to Handout 6.2: Aminoglycosides Tetracyclines • Tetracyclines are broad-spectrum antibiotics - bacteriostatic • The group includes tetracycline, oxytetracycline, doxycycline and minocycline. Clinical Use/Indication of Tetracyclines • Broad-spectrum antibiotics • The spectrum of antimicrobial activity of the tetracyclines is very wide and includes Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria Mechanism of Action of Tetracyclines • Bacteriostatic • Inhibit protein synthesis by binding specifically to the 30S ribosome • Prevents the addition of amino acid to the growing peptide chain Pharmacokinetic Aspects of Tetracyclines • All tetracyclines are adequately but incompletely absorbed from the GIT. • The percent of an oral dose that is absorbed (when the stomach is empty) is lowest for chlortetracycline (30%) and highest for minocycline (98-100%). • Most absorption takes place from the stomach and upper small intestine (greater in a fasting state). • Absorption of tetracycline is impaired by food in the stomach, milk products, and aluminium hydroxide - preparations. They should be taken at least half an hour before meal. • They have short half lives except doxycycline which can be administered once or twice daily (Tetracycline T 1⁄2 = 6hrs, Doxycycline T 1⁄2 = 16hrs and Minocycline 15hrs). • Doxycycline is virtually completely absorbed even with food. • Doxycycline does not accumulate in patients with renal failure so it can be of value in treatment of patient with impaired renal function. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 31 Drug Interactions • Anti acids, oral iron, divalent and trivalent cations (Mg2+, Ca2+, Al3+) and dairy products reduce the absorption of tetracyclines. • Barbiturates, carbamazepine and phenytoin reduce plasma concentration of doxycyclines. • Oral tetracyclines reduce the effect of combined oral contraceptives, but to a less extent. Contraindication • It is contraindicated in renal failure, pregnancy and breast feeding women and children less than 12 years. Dosage • The adult oral dose is 250mg increased in severe infection to 500mg every 6-8 hours. Unwanted Effects • The most common unwanted effects are GIT disturbances caused initially by direct irritation and later by modification of the gut flora (e.g. nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea) • Because they chelate Ca2+, tetracyclines are deposited in growing bones and teeth, causing staining and sometimes dental hypoplasia and bone deformities. They should not be given to children under 12 years, pregnant women or nursing mothers. • Another hazard to pregnant women is hepatotoxicity. • High doses of tetracyclines can decrease protein synthesis in host cells, an anti anabolic effect that may result in renal damage. • Long-term therapy can cause disturbances of the bone marrow. • Headache and visual disturbances are signs of intracranial hypertension. Discontinue from using the medication if these occurs. Other Tetracycline Formulations • Tetracycline and oxytetracycline are also available as creams/ointments for treatment bacterial skin infections. • Ointments for eye infections are also available but with different concentration as compared to those used for skin. Refer to: • Handout 6.3: Tetracyclines • Handout 6.4: Macrolides Antibiotics • Handout 6.5: Chloramphenicol Antibiotics • Handout 6.6: Quinolones Activity: Case Study Instructions Refer to Worksheet 6.1: Choice of Suitable Medicines. You will work in small groups. Read the activity instructions on the worksheet. You have 15 minutes to discuss and complete the activity. One group will be asked to present their response, so be prepared to share your work with the class. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 32 Key Points • Tetracyclines are bacteriostatic, broad-spectrum antibiotics and they chelate calcium and are deposited in growing bone and are contraindicated in children under 12 years and pregnant women. • Chloramphenicol is bacteriostatic, broad-spectrum antibiotic and the serious toxic effect is bone marrow depression, grey baby syndrome. • Aminoglycosides are bactericidal, broad-spectrum antibiotics and the unwanted side effects are dose-related nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity. • Macrolides are bactericidal/ bacteriostatic. • Ciprofloxacin has a wide antibacterial spectrum, including many organisms resistant to penicillins, cephalosporins and aminoglycosides. Evaluation • List antibiotics which act as protein synthesis inhibitors. References • Bennett, P.N. (2003). Clinical pharmacology (9th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. • British Medical Association and Royal Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain (2008). British National Formulary (BNF) No. 56.UK: Pharmaceutical Press. • Hardman, J.G. et al. (2001): Goodman and Gilman’s: The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics (10th ed.). New York: McGraw Hill. • Laurence, D. R. et al. (1997): Clinical Pharmacology (8th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. • MOHSW (2007). Standard Treatment Guidelines (STG’s) and The National Essential Drug List for Tanzania, NEDLIST (2nd ed.). Dar es salaam, Tanzania: Ministry of Health and Social Welfare. • Njau, E. (2002). Pharmacology and therapeutics (2nd ed.). African Medical and Research Foundation. • Rang, H. & Dale, M. (2007). Pharmacology. (6th ed.). Edinburgh: Churchill Livingstone. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 33 Handout 6.1: Before Starting Therapy Before starting therapy the following precepts should be considered: • Viral infections should not be treated with antibacterial. However, antibacterial are occasionally helpful in controlling secondary bacterial infection • Knowledge of prevalent organisms and their current sensitivity is of great help in choosing an antibacterial before bacteriological confirmation is available. Generally, narrow-spectrum antibacterial are preferred to broad-spectrum antibacterial unless there is a clear clinical indication • The dose of an antibacterial varies according to a number of factors including age, weight, hepatic function, renal function, and severity of infection. • An antibacterial with a narrow margin between the toxic and therapeutic dose (e.g. an aminoglycosides) it is also important to avoid an excessive dose and the concentration of the drug in the plasma may need to be monitored • The route of administration of an antibacterial often depends on the severity of the infection. Life-threatening infections require intravenous therapy. Antibacterial that are well absorbed may be given by mouth even for some serious infections. Parenteral administration is also appropriate when the oral route cannot be used (e.g. because of vomiting) or if absorption is inadequate. Whenever possible, painful intramuscular injections should be avoided in children • Duration of therapy depends on the nature of the infection and the response to treatment. Courses should not be unduly prolonged because they encourage resistance, they may lead to side-effects and they are costly. However, in certain infections such as tuberculosis or chronic osteomyelitis it is necessary to treat them with long duration of treatment. CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 34 Handout 6.2: Aminoglycosides AMIKACIN • Amikacin is more stable than gentamicin to enzyme inactivation. • Amikacin is used in the treatment of serious infections caused by gentamicin-resistant Gram-negative bacilli. • Indications: Serious Gram-negative infections resistant to gentamicin • Cautions: Same as for Gentamicin • Contraindications: Same as for Gentamicin • Unwanted effects: Same as for Gentamicin • Dose: By IM or by slow IV or by infusion, 15 mg/kg daily in 2 divided doses, increased to 22.5 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses in severe infections; max. 1.5 g daily for up to 10 days (max. cumulative dose 15 g), child 15 mg/kg daily in 2 divided doses. NEOMYCIN • Neomycin is too toxic for parenteral administration and can only be used for infections of the skin or mucous membranes or to reduce the bacterial population of the colon prior to bowel surgery or in hepatic failure. • Oral administration may lead to malabsorption. • Indication: Bowel sterilization before surgery, see also notes above, also effective for ear/eye infection • Cautions: Same as for Gentamicin, but too toxic for systemic use • Contraindications: Indications- intestinal obstruction renal impairment • Unwanted effects: Same as for Gentamicin, but poorly absorbed on oral administration, increased salivation, stomatitis, impaired intestinal absorption with diarrhoea. • Dose: By mouth, pre-operative bowel sterilization, 1g every hour for 4 hours, then 1g every 4 hours for 2–3 days CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 35 Handout 6.3: Tetracyclines DOXYCYCLINE • Uses: Doxycycline is an antibiotic used to treat a wide variety of bacterial infections • Instructions o Take each dose with a full glass of water o Do not lie down for at least 1 hour after taking this drug o Take with food or milk if stomach upset occurs o Avoid taking antacids containing magnesium, aluminium or calcium, sucralfate, iron preparations or vitamin (zinc) products within 2-3 hours of taking this medication. These products bind with the medicine preventing its absorption. • Unwanted Effects o This medication may cause stomach upset, diarrhoea, nausea, headache or vomiting. o If these symptoms persist or worsen, notify your doctor. o Very unlikely but report if stomach pain, yellowing eyes and skin or vision or mental changes have been documented • Dose o 200 mg on first day, then 100 mg daily o severe infections (including refractory urinary-tract infections), 200 mg daily which can be given in divided doses o Early syphilis, 100 mg twice daily for 14 days, latent syphilis 200 mg twice daily for 28 days o Uncomplicated genital Chlamydia o Non-gonococcal urethritis, 100 mg twice daily for 7 days OXYTETRACYCLINE • For use, side-effects, interaction and contra-indication see under Tetracyclines • Dose: 250–500 mg every 6 hours MINOCYCLINE • Indications: See under Tetracyclines, but also effective in meningococcal carrier state • Dose: 100 mg twice daily • Instructions: Tablets or capsules should be swallowed whole with plenty of fluid while sitting or standing CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 36 Handout 6.4: Macrolides Antibiotics MACROLIDES ANTIBIOTICS • Erythromycin • Clarithromycin • Azithromycin Mechanism of Action • Binds irreversibly to a site on the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome, thus inhibiting the translocation steps of protein synthesis. • Bacteriostatic, they may be Bacteriocidal at higher doses. Pharmacokinetic Aspects • Erythromycin: Incompletely but adequately absorbed, sensitive to Gastric acid; administered as enteric-coated tablets • Clarithromycin: Acid stable, oral dose rapidly absorbed. • Erythromycin -distributes readily in intracellular bodily fluids and tissues except brain and CSF ERYTHROMYCIN • Clinical uses o It has an antibacterial spectrum that is similar but not identical to that of penicillin o An alternative in penicillin-allergic patients o Active against many penicillin-resistant staphylococci but some are now also resistant to erythromycin o Also effective in bacterial skin infections • Dosage o Dose for adults by mouth is 500mg every 6 hours or 500-1000mg every 12hours o For children 2-8 years, 250mg every 6 hours o For children up to two years, 125mg every 6hours o In severe infections the dose should be doubled • Unwanted Effects o Causes GIT Upsets, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhoea in some patients o In mild to moderate infections this can be avoided by giving a lower dose (250 mg4 times daily) o If a more serious infection, such as legionella pneumonia, higher doses are needed o Hypersensivity reactions, and elevated serum bilirubin occur rarely • Contraindications o It is contraindicated in liver disease • Drug interactions o Erythromycin potentiates the action of warfarin, theophyline and carbamazepine • Other formulations o Also available as oral suspension o Creams/ointment is also available for treatment of bacterial skin infections CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 37 AZITHROMYCIN • Clinical uses o A macrolide with slightly less activity than erythromycin against Gram-positive bacteria but enhanced activity against some Gram-negative organisms including H. influenzae. o Plasma concentrations are very low but tissue concentrations are much higher. o It has a long tissue half-life and once daily dosage is recommended. o For treatment of Lyme disease, and in the treatment of trachoma [unlicensed indication] CLARITHROMYCIN • Clinical uses o An erythromycin derivative with slightly greater activity than the parent compound o Tissue concentrations are higher than with erythromycin • Dose o It is given twelve hourly (twice daily) • Unwanted effects o Azithromycin and clarithromycin cause fewer gastro-intestinal side-effects and P450 drug interactions than erythromycin CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 38 Handout 6.5: Chloramphenicol Antibiotics CHLORAMPHENICOL • Chloramphenicol has a wide spectrum of antimicrobial activity, including Gram-negative and Gram-positive organisms and rickettsiae. • It is bacteriostatic for most organisms but kills H.influenzae. • Clinical uses: o Chloramphenicol is a potent broad-spectrum antibiotic o However, it is associated with serious haematological side-effects when given systemically and should therefore be reserved for the treatment of life-threatening infections, particularly those caused by Haemophilus influenzae.  Such uses may include: infections caused by Haemophilus influenzae resistant to other drugs and meningitis in patients whom penicillin cannot be used  It is also safe and effective in bacterial conjunctivitis (given topically)  It is effective in typhoid fever, but ciprofloxacin is similarly effective at a lower dose and less toxic • Pharmacokinetic aspects o Given orally, it is rapidly and completely absorbed and reaches its maximum concentration in the plasma within 2 hours o It can also be given parenterally o The drug is widely distributed throughout the tissues and body fluids including the CSF, where its concentration may reach 60% of that in the blood o In the plasma, it is 30-50% protein-bound, and its half-life is approximately 2 hours o About 10% is excreted unchanged in the urine, and the remainder is inactivated in the liver • Unwanted effects o Bone marrow depression leading to blood disorders including reversible and irreversible aplastic anaemia (with reports of resulting leukaemia) o Peripheral neuritis, optic neuritis, headache, urticaria, erythema multiforme, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, stomatitis, glossitis, dry mouth, nocturnal haemoglobinuria reported o Grey syndrome (abdominal distension, pallid cyanosis, circulatory collapse) may follow excessive doses in neonates with immature hepatic metabolism. • Dose o By mouth or by intravenous injection or infusion o Adult 50 mg/kg body weight daily in 4 divided doses o Neonate under 2 weeks 25 mg/kg daily (in 4 divided doses) o Infant 2 weeks–1 year 50 mg/kg daily (in 4 divided doses) • Contraindications o Use extreme caution when giving this drug to children less than 2 years old o This drug should be used only when clearly needed during pregnancy o Discuss the risks and benefits with your patient • Drug Interactions o It increases the action of anticoagulants and decreases the production of Vitamin K • Other preparations o Chloramphenicol eye drops and chloramphenicol ear drops are also available CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 39 Handout 6.6: Quinolones The quinolones include the broad-spectrum agents ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin and norfloxacin as well as a narrow-spectrum drug used in urinary tract infections, a synthetic fluorinated analogs nalidixic acid. Indications • Ciprofloxacin is the most commonly used fluoroquinolones and will be described as the type agent • A broad-spectrum antibiotic effective against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms • Excellent activity against the Enterobacteriaceae (the enteric Gram-negative bacilli), including many organisms resistant to penicillins, cephalosporins and aminoglycosides • Also effective against H.influenzae, penicillinases-producing N.gonorrhoeae, Campylobacter sp. and pseudomonas. • Of the Gram-positive organisms, streptococci and pneumococcal are only weakly inhibited, and there is a high incidence of staphylococcal resistance Modes of Action • These agents inhibit topoisomerase II (a bacterial DNA gyrase), the enzyme that produces a negative super coil in DNA and thus permits transcription or replication • They are bactericidal, and exhibit a concentration dependent bacterial killing Pharmacokinetic aspects • Given orally, the fluoroquinolones are well absorbed. • The half-life of ciprofloxacin and norfloxacin is 3 hours, and that of ofloxacin is 5 hours. • All quinolones are concentrated in phagocytes. Most fail to cross the blood-brain barrier, but ofloxacin penetrates the CSF and attains 40% of its serum concentrations. • Aluminium and magnesium antacids, calcium, and dairy products interfere with the absorption of the quinolones. • Elimination of ciprofloxacin and norfloxacin is partly by hepatic metabolism by P450 enzymes and partly by renal excretion. • Ofloxacin is excreted in the urine Unwanted Effects • Unwanted effects are infrequent, usually mild, and disappear when the drugs are withdrawn • Most frequent manifestations are gastrointestinal disorders and skin rashes CIPROFLOXACIN Cautions • Avoid excessive alkalinity of urine and ensure adequate fluid intake • May impair performance of skilled tasks (e.g. driving) • Effects enhanced by alcohol • Unwanted effects CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 40 o Flatulence, pain and phlebitis at injection site o Rarely dysphagia, pancreatitis, chest pain, tachycardia, syncope, oedema, hot flushes, abnormal dreams, sweating, hyperglycaemia, and erythema nodosum o Very rarely movement disorders tinnitus and Achilles tendon rupture Dose • By mouth, respiratory-tract infections, 250–750 mg twice daily • Urinary-tract infections, 250–500 mg twice daily • Gonorrhea, 500 mg twice daily • Most other infections, 500–750 mg twice daily • Child and adolescent not recommended but where benefit outweighs risk, by mouth, 5– 15 mg/kg (max. 750 mg) twice daily or by intravenous infusion, 4–8 mg/kg (max. 400 mg) twice daily • Ciprofloxacin is also available as eye/ear drops solution NALIDIXIC ACID • Indications: Urinary-tract infections • Cautions: Avoid in porphyria, liver disease and renal disease • Side-effects: Nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, headache, diarrhoea, toxic psychosis, increased intracranial pressure, cranial nerve palsy, metabolic acidosis and haemolysis • Dose: 1000mg every 6 hours for 7 days, reduced in chronic infections to 600 mg every 6 hours, child over 3 months max. 50 mg/kg daily in divided doses; reduced in prolonged therapy to 30 mg/kg daily NITROFURANTOIN • Indications o Nitrofurantoin is used as a urinary antiseptic o It is less expensive than Nalidixic acid o It is concentrated in the urine and therefore there is no systemic antibacterial effect • Dosage: 5-8mg/kg body weight by mouth in 3-4 daily divided doses • Unwanted effects: GIT upsets including nausea, and vomiting, anorexia, peripheral neuropathy and transient alopecia, haemolytic anaemia, pulmonary fibrosis • Contraindication: Nitrofurantoin should not be given to patients with impaired renal function G6PG deficiency • Interaction: Probenecid reduces its excretion CMT 05109 Pharmacology and Therapeutic NTA Level 5 Semester 1 Student Manual Session 6: Protein Synthesis Inhibitor Antibiotics 41 Worksheet 6.1: Choice of Suitable Medicines Case Study Instructions"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-824877ba9a22","stem":"Discuss the case together and answer the related questions in the time you are given. Scenario A patient has a urinary-tract infection and is complaining of nausea in early pregnancy. A urinary culture and sensitivity test was done and the organism is reported as being resistant to ampicillin but sensitive to gentamicin, tetracycline, trimethoprim and cefalexin. Question What will be a suitable anti-bacterial?","type":"Essay / Structured Essay","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-84969d1c831f","stem":"How is the Module Organized?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b7bb67ac2f0b","stem":"How Should the Module be Used?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-73dfbf151faa","stem":"Which of the following is NOT Gram-positive?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Listeria monocytogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus"},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella pneumonia"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eac56280cdf6","stem":"Which of the following is rod shaped?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"label":"B","text":"Niesseria gonorrhoeae"},{"label":"C","text":"Bacillus anthracis"},{"label":"D","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi"},{"label":"E","text":"Moraxella catarrhalis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-75dfb65a7996","stem":"Which of the following is the best description of listeria monocytogenes?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram-negative spirals"},{"label":"B","text":"Gram-negative cocci"},{"label":"C","text":"Gram-negative rods"},{"label":"D","text":"Gram-positive cocci"},{"label":"E","text":"Gram-positive rods"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4e4af3940e41","stem":"Which of the following would be the best magnification power for viewing bacteria such as Echerichia coli with an oil immersion microscope?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1x"},{"label":"B","text":"10x"},{"label":"C","text":"100x"},{"label":"D","text":"1,000x"},{"label":"E","text":"10,000x Lab 2 – Gram-positive Cocci"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a45b7aaf10cf","stem":"Which of the following tests would involve looking for “bubbling” after application of the test agent?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Bacterial identification","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Catalase"},{"label":"B","text":"Coagulase"},{"label":"C","text":"Bile esculin"},{"label":"D","text":"Bacitracin"},{"label":"E","text":"Optochin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"independent_checked","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"checked_explained","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9743d4407eef","stem":"If a bacteria ferments mannitol salt, which is a peachy red color, what color will it change to if the test is positive?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blue"},{"label":"B","text":"Red"},{"label":"C","text":"Green"},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow"},{"label":"E","text":"Black"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3856fa53b274","stem":"Streptococci pneumoniae is the number one cause of all of the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adult meningitis"},{"label":"B","text":"Neonatal meningitis"},{"label":"C","text":"Community acquired pneumonia"},{"label":"D","text":"Otitis media in children"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option C"],"duplicateStemGroup":"73fb763ecdd8f926d2c722588eaa47986c52174c"},{"id":"q-5a2cc0d8850f","stem":"In the hemolysis test on a blood agar plate, an incomplete hemolysis will show streaks of what color?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blue"},{"label":"B","text":"Clear"},{"label":"C","text":"Green"},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow"},{"label":"E","text":"Black"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d534a7de308d","stem":"Which of the following Gram-positive cocci is catalase positive and coagulase positive?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococci epidermidis"},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococci saprophyticus"},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococci aureus"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococci pyogenes"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococci agalactiae"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a856ae4348b2","stem":"Which of the following Gram-positive cocci is γ (gamma) hemolytic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococci pneumonia"},{"label":"B","text":"Viridans strep"},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococci pyogenes"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococci agalactiae"},{"label":"E","text":"Enterococcus Lab 3 – Gram-negative Bacteria"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d666961bc2f","stem":"Which of the following Gram-negative rods does not ferment glucose and is oxidase positive?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"E. coli"},{"label":"B","text":"P. vulgaris"},{"label":"C","text":"P. aeruginosa"},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonella"},{"label":"E","text":"Shigella"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9c2ff63b8e80","stem":"In the lactose fermentation MacConkey test, what color would indicate a positive test?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Black"},{"label":"B","text":"Pink"},{"label":"C","text":"Blue"},{"label":"D","text":"Purple"},{"label":"E","text":"Green"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a835b4bb61e1","stem":"Which of the following Gram-negative rods ferments glucose, is oxidase negative, ferments lactose, and is indole positive (tryptophan metabolizing)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Carbohydrate Metabolism","topicOriginal":"Carbohydrate Metabolism","subtopic":"Glucose","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"E. coli"},{"label":"B","text":"Shigella"},{"label":"C","text":"K. pneumoniae"},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonella"},{"label":"E","text":"P. vulgaris"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Carbohydrate Metabolism","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8bbfa6647255","stem":"A child presents with vomiting and bloody stools. A bacterial culture yields Gram- negative rods that are H2S negative. Which of the following is most likely?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"E. coli"},{"label":"B","text":"Shigella"},{"label":"C","text":"K. pneumoniae"},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonella"},{"label":"E","text":"P. vulgaris"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleani