nswer)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teichoic acids and lipoteichoic acids"},{"label":"B","text":"Periplasmic space and LPS"},{"label":"C","text":"Teichoic acids and porins"},{"label":"D","text":"Teichoic acids, lipoteichoic acids, and porins"},{"label":"E","text":"Periplasmic space, LPS, and porins"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb3be502c30c","stem":"1) Which of the following best describes Gram-negative bacteria?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thick layer of peptidoglycan and no outer membrane"},{"label":"B","text":"Thick layer of peptidoglycan and an outer membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"Thin layer of peptidoglycan and no outer membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"Thin layer of peptidoglycan and an outer membrane"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3b403176cc3b","stem":"2) The peptidoglycan forms a meshlike layer around the cell, consisting of a polysaccharide polymer cross-linked by what kind of bonds?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Van der Waals"},{"label":"B","text":"Peptide"},{"label":"C","text":"Electrostatic"},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfide"},{"label":"E","text":"Hydrogen"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fde341be9908","stem":"3) The cross-linked bonds are between a terminal D-____ from one chain and a diamino amino acid from the other chain. A pentaglycine bridge (gly5) expands the cross-link in Staphylococcus aureus.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alanine"},{"label":"B","text":"Valine"},{"label":"C","text":"Proline"},{"label":"D","text":"Arginine"},{"label":"E","text":"Tryptophan"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d3ef45ea1286","stem":"1) Which of the following functions in mating and adhesion to host cells?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pili"},{"label":"B","text":"Capsules"},{"label":"C","text":"Flagella"},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmids"},{"label":"E","text":"Outer membrane"}],"priority":2,"stat