) In the second phase of peptidoglycan synthesis, the pentapeptide is attached to the bactoprenol “conveyor belt” in the cytoplasmic membrane. What is then added to make the disaccharide building block of the peptidoglycan?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"AlaNAc"},{"label":"B","text":"AlaNAla"},{"label":"C","text":"GlcNAc"},{"label":"D","text":"GluNAc"},{"label":"E","text":"GluNAla"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-91eab04f1279","stem":"3) In the third phase of peptidoglycan synthesis, where is the disaccharide:peptide precursor moved to?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytoplasm"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitochondria"},{"label":"C","text":"Ribosomes"},{"label":"D","text":"Outside of the cell"},{"label":"E","text":"Inside the nucleus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-460af3932732","stem":"What bacterial cell component is affected by cephalosporin, penicillin, and other β- lactam antibiotics?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipopolysaccharide"},{"label":"B","text":"DD-Carboxypeptidase"},{"label":"C","text":"Transpeptidases (PBPs)"},{"label":"D","text":"β-Lactamase"},{"label":"E","text":"Autolysins"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9d37a41b82df","stem":"Some G+, but never G-, bacteria form spores. These include members of the genera","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacillus and Clostridium"},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococcus and Streptococcus"},{"label":"C","text":"Bacillus and Streptococcus"},{"label":"D","text":"Clostridium and Staphylococcus"},{"label":"E","text":"Bacillus and Staphylococcus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option A"],"duplicateStemGroup":"92c8320f49f818db87afc87a671dee226253f9c6"},{"id":"q-340b86d8978b","stem":"Which of the following is NOT true regarding spores?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They can allow bacteria to exist in suspended animation for centuries"},{"label":"B","text":"It contains a high concentration of calcium bound to dipicolinic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"It contains a complete copy of the chromosomes"},{"label":"D","text":"It contains high concentrations of the essential proteins and ribosomes"},{"label":"E","text":"It protects the DNA from intense heat, radiation, and attack by most enzymes 4 – Bacterial Metabolism and Growth"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-db03d588fbf0","stem":"What is the initial phase of bacterial growth (on the bacterial growth curve) called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decline"},{"label":"B","text":"Stationary phase"},{"label":"C","text":"Log phase"},{"label":"D","text":"Exponential phase"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e08e746c1439","stem":"Clostridium perfringens cannot grow with oxygen present and thus is a(n) ____ while Mycobacterium tuberculosis requires oxygen and thus is a(n) ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Respiratory Medicine","topicOriginal":"Respiratory Medicine","subtopic":"Tuberculosis","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obligate anaerobe; Obligate aerobe"},{"label":"B","text":"Obligate aerobe; Obligate anaerobe"},{"label":"C","text":"Obligate anaerobe; Obligate anaerobe"},{"label":"D","text":"Obligate aerobe; Obligate aerobe"},{"label":"E","text":"Facultative anaerobe; Facultative anaerobe"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Respiratory Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7fc5a9e1b594","stem":"At the onset of bacterial transcription, what allows for the recognition of a particular sequence of nucleotides in the DNA (promoter)? Some bacteria encode several of these to allow transcription of a group of genes under special conditions, such as heat shock, starvation, special nitrogen metabolism, or sporulation.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Operon"},{"label":"B","text":"Epsilon factor"},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha factor"},{"label":"D","text":"Sigma factor"},{"label":"E","text":"Delta factor"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-59d6fce12418","stem":"Which of the following best describes the function of fMet-tRNA?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protein folding into the tertiary structure"},{"label":"B","text":"Initiating transcription allowing for RNA copying"},{"label":"C","text":"Attaching to P-site during translation allowing for 70S assembly"},{"label":"D","text":"Attaching to A-site during translation allowing for 30S binding"},{"label":"E","text":"Attaching to the stop codon during translation to release the protein 5 – Bacterial Genetics Match the following descriptions with their terms:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-191fc4c768e2","stem":"By what mechanism do pathogenicity (virulence) islands coordinate the expression of a system of virulence factors?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Replicons"},{"label":"B","text":"Cistrons"},{"label":"C","text":"Polycistronic"},{"label":"D","text":"Transposons"},{"label":"E","text":"Episomal"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f6a18535263","stem":"1) Which of the following is the process by which bacteria take up fragments of naked DNA and incorporate them into their genomes?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conjuga