"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-db777772d167","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a classic flulike symptom associated with viral infections?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fever"},{"label":"B","text":"Malaise"},{"label":"C","text":"Headache"},{"label":"D","text":"Body aches"},{"label":"E","text":"Binging Match the following descriptions with their terms:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7e7785f19ad9","stem":"1) Which of the following is NOT an effective means to prevent viral transmission?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Education"},{"label":"B","text":"Universal precautions"},{"label":"C","text":"Improved hygiene"},{"label":"D","text":"Antibiotics"},{"label":"E","text":"Ensuring all personnel are immunized against common diseases"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-64339036b181","stem":"2) According to the U.S. Center of Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), what is the “single most important means of preventing the spread of infection?”","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gloves"},{"label":"B","text":"Gown"},{"label":"C","text":"Mask"},{"label":"D","text":"Hand washing"},{"label":"E","text":"The “five second rule” 22 – Staphylococcus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-895fb91db08f","stem":"Which of the following describes the Gram reaction and morphology of Staphylococcus?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"G- rods"},{"label":"B","text":"G- cocci"},{"label":"C","text":"G+ rods"},{"label":"D","text":"G+ cocci"},{"label":"E","text":"G- spirals Match the virulence factors with its biological effect:"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"newly_reasoned_provisional","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-409e582a2979","stem":"What is the most common reservoir site of S. aureus?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"UG tract"},{"label":"B","text":"Enteric"},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphoid"},{"label":"D","text":"Liver"},{"label":"E","text":"Skin and mucous membranes"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a7f64323effd","stem":"1) A patient undergoes a procedure to implant a cardiac pacemaker and prosthetic heart valve. One month later, the patient returns with a high fever. Infection is found in the pacemaker pocket, along the pathway of the electrical lead, and near the prosthetic valve. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus"},{"label":"D","text":"Viridans Streptococci"},{"label":"E","text":"E. Coli"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"7b6c15c14d08277e779efa5c7574989652441733"},{"id":"q-4e7975b20c0d","stem":"2) Which of the following diseases would be causes by the toxic activity of Staphylococcus aureus and not the proliferation activity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Food poisoning"},{"label":"B","text":"Meningitis"},{"label":"C","text":"Osteomyelitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Septic arthritis"},{"label":"E","text":"Acute endocarditis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9aee63df10a","stem":"3) Which of the following is associated with Ritter syndrome in neonates, where it colonizes the cut umbilicus and releases ET-A,B systematically?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"label":"B","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus"},{"label":"D","text":"Viridans Streptococci"},{"label":"E","text":"E. Coli"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5e9cd51b0c01","stem":"4) A sexually active woman develops dysuria, pyuria, and fever suggestive of urinary tract infection. Urine cultures show G+ bacteria in clusters that are catalase positive and coagulase negative. E. Coli is ruled out as a cause of the cystitis. Which of the following would be the most likely cause of the cystitis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viridans Streptococci"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"label":"E","text":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus Match the following descriptions with their terms:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-180e7178fbef","stem":"Which of the following would be the best choice of antibiotic (currently) for a strain of Staphylococcus aureus that has acquired the mecA gene?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methicillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Nafcillin"},{"label":"C","text":"Oxacillin"},{"label":"D","text":"Dicloxacillin"},{"label":"E","text":"Vancomycin 23 – Streptococci"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3a24a36e4624","stem":"Which of the following tests would differentiate between Staphylococci and Streptococci, where the former is positive and the later is negative?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"Bile esculin"},{"label":"C","text":"Catalase"},{"label":"D","text":"Coagulase"},{"label":"E","text":"Optochin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-90e410213584","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a classification schema used to identify Streptococci?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teichoic acids"},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolytic patterns"},{"label":"C","text":"Biochemical (physiologic) properties"},{"label":"D","text":"Serologic properties (Lancefield groupings) Match the following Streptococcal pathogens with their hemolytic pattern:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b5a53f7857f1","stem":"1) A middle-aged woman presents with low-grade fever and general malaise. Physical exam reveals Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, and splinter hemorrhages under her fingernails. Echocardiogram indicates vegetations on the mitral valve. In the doctor’s office, she recounts a dentist appointment a few weeks ago and several bouts of sore throat as a child. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c64f6c76d58b","stem":"2) A young child presents with fever and a skin rash localized around the lips on his arms. The rash appears pustular with yellow crusts. Cultures from the impetigo show Gram-positive cocci in chains that are β-hemolytic. The doctor administers penicillin G and warns the parents that the child may develop transient smokey-colored urine soon. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-27a34f3fc4a3","stem":"3) An old man develops a UTI five-days after admission to the hospital. His record indicates that he is receiving antibiotic treatment including cephalosporins for an unrelated infection. In treating the patient, physicians check for resistance to vancomycin. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"64f123e454e905841b3efc1f3981b3be4134bfa1"},{"id":"q-d1f0a6c65736","stem":"4) An elderly man develops low-grade fever and signs of endocarditis over a period of 2 weeks. Following blood culture, his doctor also becomes concerned about possible colon cancer. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fc94b6871460","stem":"5) An elderly woman presents with a cough producing rusty-colored sputum. She complains of sharp right-sided chest pains, chills, and fevers. Physical exam reveals increased fremitus, dullness to percussion, and bronchial breath sounds on the lower right side. CXR shows right lower lobe consolidation, and Gram stains of sputum show Gram- positive diplococci. Physicians begin treatment with cephalosporins. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-54c97021200e","stem":"6) Soon after birth, an infant develops seizures, a marked irritability, poor feeding, and fever. The infant’s birth records note a prolonged labor with premature rupture of membranes. A lumbar puncture was done and the infant was started on antibiotics. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c63ade8b0b72","stem":"7) Which of the following is the most common cause of pneumonia in adults (40-65 years), the most common causes of pneumonia in the elderly (> 65 years), and the most common cause of meningitis in the elderly (> 60 years)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-497608560541","stem":"8) Which of the following is the most common cause of neonatal meningitis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option C"],"duplicateStemGroup":"cb2cf0c093430801a0468274dec112e3fdbd12d3"},{"id":"q-1b0f0eceffd5","stem":"9) Which of the following is associated with glomerulonephritis and rheumatic fever (Jones Criteria for Diagnosis)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2e1d528681c2","stem":"10) Of the following five most common pediatric diseases with a rash, which one is associated with Streptococcus pyogenes?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measles (fever of three days with cough, coryza, or conjunctivitis)"},{"label":"B","text":"Rubella (Forchheimer sign, fever, swollen glands)"},{"label":"C","text":"Scarlet fever (“strawberry tongue”, “sandpaper rash”)"},{"label":"D","text":"Roseola (high fever, “rose red rash”)"},{"label":"E","text":"Erythema infectiosum (bright red cheeks)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-376325806ae5","stem":"M Protein is a virulence factor that binds to H factor, causing C3B to be degraded, and protecting the organism from being phagocytized. Which of the following is associated with M Protein?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae"},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococcus faecalis"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcus bovis"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"label":"F","text":"Streptococcus mutans (Viridans Streptococci group)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0855fafe6062","stem":"Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins (Spes) act as superantigens and are associated with which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dental caries and brain abscesses"},{"label":"B","text":"Subacute Bacterial Endocarditis (SBE)"},{"label":"C","text":"Toxic Shock Syndrome (TSS) and Scarlet fever"},{"label":"D","text":"Pharyngitis and impetigo"},{"label":"E","text":"Glomerulonephritis and rheumatic fever"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-519c70c496d3","stem":"The measurement of antibodies against streptolysin O (the ASO test) is useful for confirming which of the following streptococcal infection symptoms, 3 to 4 weeks or more after the initial exposure to the organism?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dental caries and brain abscesses"},{"label":"B","