":"Paroxysmal (2-4 weeks)"},{"label":"D","text":"Convalescent (3-4 weeks or longer)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b38d987f015d","stem":"2) In B. pertussis infection, where is the number of organisms the highest?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"CSF"},{"label":"B","text":"GI tract"},{"label":"C","text":"UG tract"},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory tract"},{"label":"E","text":"Skin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-d38249d67985","stem":"3) An infant born in a rural area is brought to the ER with severe bouts of coughing throughout the day. During the visit, the baby appears cyanotic and suffers an attack of many coughs on a single expiration followed by a deep inspiration. The cough produces copious greenish phlegm. Further history reveals that the infant has not been vaccinated. The physician fears that erythromycin therapy will not be helpful at this stage of the illness. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"S. pneumoniae"},{"label":"B","text":"P. aeruginosa"},{"label":"C","text":"B. pertussis"},{"label":"D","text":"F. tularensis"},{"label":"E","text":"L. pneumophila"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b82882fbfdb5","stem":"The ____ version of the pertussis vaccine is associated with unacceptable levels of complications. The ____ version of the vaccine contains inactivated bacteria and the ____ version of the vaccine contains the pertussis toxin and one (or more) bacterial components, which mediate immune response in the host.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whole cell; Whole cell; Acellular"},{"label":"B","text":"Whole cell; Acellular; Whole cell"},{"label":"C","text":"Acellular; Whole cell; Acellular"},{"label":"D","text":"Acellular; Acellular; Whole cell"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option A"],"duplicateStemGroup":"bdc44883e26c0f849641cf50fcd8b122a1acef98"},{"id":"q-86167bed1262","stem":"Brucella and Francisella are examples of what kind of disease, where they can be transmitted from animal to human?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganis