on","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endocarditis"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes"},{"label":"C","text":"Neoplasms"},{"label":"D","text":"Osteomyelitis"},{"label":"E","text":"Chagas disease 42 – Anaerobic Gram-Negative Bacilli Match the following descriptions with the anaerobic gram-negative bacilli:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-71dc5e92d80d","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a virulence factor of Bacteroides fragilis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polysaccharide capsule allow for adhesion"},{"label":"B","text":"Capsule protects from phagocytosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Superoxide dismutase prevent oxygen toxicity"},{"label":"D","text":"Diarrheal disease caused by heat-labile toxin"},{"label":"E","text":"LPS causes shock"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d14167f9b090","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a difficulty associated with laboratory diagnosis of anaerobic bacteria?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contamination of sample with normal bacterial population"},{"label":"B","text":"Drying of the sample causes significant bacterial loss"},{"label":"C","text":"Slow growth of over two weeks causes delay"},{"label":"D","text":"Staining is faint and irregular"},{"label":"E","text":"Transport must be in oxygen free environment"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-de48ea9a43ff","stem":"1) Which of the following is NOT one of the most common anaerobes found in brain abscess infections?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"B. fagillis"},{"label":"B","text":"Prevotella"},{"label":"C","text":"Porphyromonas"},{"label":"D","text":"Fusobacterium"},{"label":"E","text":"Peptostreptococcus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-59a4648fce6a","stem":"4) Which of the following is most commonly seen in intraabdominal infections and bacteremia?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"B. fagillis"},{"label":"B","text":"Prevotella"},{"label":"C","text":"Porphyromonas"},{"label":"D","text":"Fusobacterium"},{"label":"E","text":"Peptostreptococcus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a49538d4e9b2","stem":"1) What growth media is used to help differentiate B. fragilis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chocolate (CHOC)"},{"label":"B","text":"Thayer-Martin (TM)"},{"label":"C","text":"Blood agar plate (BAP)"},{"label":"D","text":"Bile Esculin agar (BEA)"},{"label":"E","text":"MacConkey agar (MAC)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3feea4b314be","stem":"2 Bacteroides are the only Gram-negative bacteria without typical","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capsule"},{"label":"B","text":"Endotoxin"},{"label":"C","text":"Adhesins"},{"label":"D","text":"Catalase"},{"label":"E","text":"Fimbriae 43 – Treponema, Borrelia and Leptospira Match the spirochetal bacteria and the disease caused by each:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ad5a9f8fccb","stem":"Which of the following is true for visualizing spirochete?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They can be viewed without significant magnification (e.g. 10x)"},{"label":"B","text":"They can be viewed with Gram stain"},{"label":"C","text":"They can be viewed with Giemsa stain"},{"label":"D","text":"They require special analysis such as dark field illumination or silver stain"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-4cfd224200a6","stem":"1) Which of the following groups of people is most at risk for contracting T. pallidum?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pregnant mothers"},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic fibrosis patients"},{"label":"C","text":"Sexually active patients"},{"label":"D","text":"Elderly patients"},{"label":"E","text":"Anyone (spread through contact with inanimate objects, such as toilet seats)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-83260e94b5d8","stem":"2) The tissue destruction and lesions observed in syphilis are primarily a consequence of which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacterial capsule"},{"label":"B","text":"Bacterial endoflagellum"},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial overgrowth"},{"label":"D","text":"Bacterial hyaluronidase"},{"label":"E","text":"Host immune response"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f3d3baa482d4","stem":"1) What stage of syphilis is characterized by disseminating rash, alopecia, lymphadenopathy, and flulike symptoms.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary"},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary"},{"label":"C","text":"Tertiary"},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ae2cf98a8300","stem":"2) Children born with congenital syphilis often exhibit Hutchinson Triad, which includes deafness, blindness, and centrally notched teeth. If the mother has been exposed to Chlamydia, syphilis, or gonorrhea, the newborn may receive a prophylactic antibiotic for their","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ears"},{"label":"B","text":"Eyes"},{"label":"C","text":"Nose"},{"label":"D","text":"Mouth"},{"label":"E","text":"Systemic antibiotic (via IV)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9c66c05ecac5","stem":"3) What is characteristic of primary syphilis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painful chancre"},{"label":"B","text":"Painless chancre"},{"label":"C","text":"Several painful ulcers in genital region"},{"label":"D","text":"Several painless ulcers in genital region"},{"label":"E","text":"Disseminating rash on entire body, soles, and palms"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd1ef14524ec","stem":"4) Of the following organisms that can cross the placenta, which of the following is associated with congenital CN VIII deafness, mulberry molars, saddle nose, blindness, deafness, and cardiovascular problems?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Embryology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxoplasma gondii"},{"label":"B","text":"Rubella"},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomegalovirus"},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes, HIV"},{"label":"E","text":"Syphilis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Embryology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option E"],"duplicateStemGroup":"d1c143ddad41641bb9f310365704c85e202e0e2e"},{"id":"q-75a7d9c47e4c","stem":"Which of the following is/are used to confirm nontreponemal assays?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venereal Disease Research Laboratory (VDRL) test"},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid Plasma Reagin (RPR) test"},{"label":"C","text":"Fluorescent treponemal antibody-absorption (FTA-ABS) test"},{"label":"D","text":"Treponema pallidum particle agglutination (TP-PA) test"},{"label":"E","text":"C & D Match the modes of transmission with the spirochete:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8d7c99720577","stem":"Which of the following is an early clinical sign of Lyme Disease and not a late sign?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Musculoskeletal disease (e.g. migratory myalgias, transient arthritis)"},{"label":"B","text":"Nervous system disease (e.g. Bell palsy, encephalopathy)"},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiovascular involvement (e.g. AV nodal block)"},{"label":"D","text":"Erythema chronicum migrans (ECM)"},{"label":"E","text":"Acrodermatitis chronicum atrophicans (ACA)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9c5d624e51db","stem":"1) Which of the following locations within the United States has the highest reported cases of Leptospira infection each year?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minnesota"},{"label":"B","text":"Pennsylvania"},{"label":"C","text":"New York"},{"label":"D","text":"Wisconsin"},{"label":"E","text":"Hawaii"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3863a1611e2d","stem":"2) A farmer comes to the ER with a 1-week history of flu-like symptoms with photophobia. His severe headache, cough, and myalgias suggest to the physician some kind of respiratory infection. However, more careful physical exam reveals conjunctival suffusion and macular rash. Lab findings include elevated serum bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, aminotransferase, and creatine phosphokinase. With this clinical picture and lab results, the physician prescribes penicillin G immediately. His suspicions are confirmed later when a spirochete is isolated from the patient’s blood. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema pallidum"},{"label":"B","text":"Leptospira interrogans"},{"label":"C","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi"},{"label":"D","text":"Borrelia recurrentis 44 – Mycoplasma and Ureaplasma"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-753fecf50719","stem":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae (Eaton agent) are unique among bacteria because their cell membrane contains sterols and they do not have which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"β-lactam antibiotic resistance"},{"label":"B","text":"Flagella"},{"label":"C","text":"Endotoxin"},{"label":"D","text":"Capsule"},{"label":"E","text":"Cell wall"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0d3312751b70","stem":"Which of the following is associated with Ureaplasma?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genitourinary tract infection"},{"label":"B","text":"Atypical pneumonia"},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheobronchitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Influenza-like illness"},{"label":"E","text":"Upper respiratory tract infection"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"Which of the following is associated with Ureaplasma? Version: 03Jun2008 Page 54 of 66 Microbiology Quiz","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"Genitourinary tract infection"},{"label":"B","text":"Atypical pneumonia"},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheobronchitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Influenza-like illness"},{"label":"E","text":"Upper respiratory tract infection"}]}},{"id":"q-8459e25c3fa2","stem":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a common cause of atypical pneumonia (walking pneumonia) and chest x-ray often looks worse than symptoms suggest. Which of the following groups of people is the most commonly affected?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elderly"},{"label":"B","text":"Middle-aged adults"},{"label":"C","text":"School-age children"},{"label":"D","text":"Neonates"},{"label":"E","text":"Pregnant mothers"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-594fa34aafb2","stem":"Which of the following tests is the most useful currently as an initial laboratory test, has about 65% sensitivity, and has specificity that cross-reacts to other organisms (e.g., Epstein-Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, adenovirus)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microscopy"},{"label":"B","text":"Molecular diagnosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Complement fixation"},{"label":"D","text":"Enzyme immunoassays"},{"label":"E","text":"Cold hemagglutination 45 – Rickettsia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a2a18617002c","stem":"1) Which of the following is an organism that prefers to live inside a host cell but may live outside a host cell?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extracellular pathogen"},{"label":"B","text":"Intracellular pathogen"},{"label":"C","text":"Obligate intracellular parasite"},{"label":"D","text":"Obligate extracellular parasite"},{"label":"E","text":"Facultative intracellular parasite"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-1e7381ebbfaa","stem":"2) Rickettsia, an aerobic Gram-negative rod, is which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extracellular pathogen"},{"label":"B","text":"Obligate intracellular parasite"},{"label":"C","text":"Obligate extracellular parasite"},{"label":"D","text":"Facultative intracellular parasite Match the following Rickettsia with the disease and location:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3dc38c4e4093","stem":"9) A 10-year-old boy in Virginia presents with a rash, fever, and severe headache that began several days ago. The rash began on his palms and soles and has now spread to his trunk. His pediatrician also notes conjunctival redness, and lab studies show proteinuria. The boy’s history is significant for a hike in the woods a week ago. The child is given tetracycline and his diagnosis is confirmed by a Weil-Felix test. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orientia tsutsugamushi"},{"label":"B","text":"Coxiella burnetii"},{"label":"C","text":"Rickettsia rickettsii"},{"label":"D","text":"Rickettsia prowazekii"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d88d7f21b6da","stem":"10) A cattle farmer goes to the doctor complaining of mild cough and fever. He says that the fever began abruptly several days ago. His occupation as cattle slaughterer leads the doctor towards a diagnosis, and tetracycline is administered. The diagnosis is confirmed by serology and a negative Weil-Felix test. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orientia tsutsugamushi"},{"label":"B","text":"Coxiella burnetii"},{"label":"C","text":"Rickettsia rickettsii"},{"label":"D","text":"Rickettsia prowazekii"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5576266904d4","stem":"11) A Kosovo refugee sees a volunteer camp doctor complaining of a rash spreading outward from his trunk but sparing his palms and soles. Two days ago, he experiences abrupt onset of fever, headaches, and confusion. On physical exam, the doctor discovers lice in the man’s hair. The doctor treats with a delousing regimen and tetracycline. Were he at a hospital, he might confirm the diagnosis with a Weil-Felix test. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orientia tsutsugamushi"},{"label":"B","text":"Coxiella burnetii"},{"label":"C","text":"Rickettsia rickettsii"},{"label":"D","text":"Rickettsia prowazekii 47 – Chlamydia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f531040a97c2","stem":"1) The Chlamydiaceae life cycle has elemental bodies (EBs) that are ____ and reticulate bodies (RBs) that are ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolically active and infection; Metabolically inactive and noninfectious"},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolically inactive and infection; Metabolically active and noninfectious"},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolically active and noninfection; Metabolically inactive and infectious"},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolically inactive and noninfection; Metabolically active and infectious"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7ecfbef4d3c9","stem":"2) In the life cycle of Chlamydiaceae, ____ bind to cell receptors triggering endocytosis. ____ inhibit lysosome fusion and form ___, which replicate by binary fission and form ____ that cause the cell to lyse.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"EBs; EBs; RBs; EBs;"},{"label":"B","text":"EBs; RBs; RBs; EBs;"},{"label":"C","text":"RBs; EBs; EBs; RBs;"},{"label":"D","text":"RBs; RBs; EBs; RBs;"},{"label":"E","text":"RBs; RBs; EBs; EBs;"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option E"],"duplicateStemGroup":"b3c5a5b560d3f93d9c2dc7a7014d378859194edb"},{"id":"q-389af64c142c","stem":"Chlamydia uses ATP for energy in order to construct DNA and proteins as well as encoding their own ribosomes. This makes Clamydia which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extracellular pathogen"},{"label":"B","text":"Obligate intracellular parasite"},{"label":"C","text":"Obligate extracellular parasite"},{"label":"D","text":"Facultative intracellular parasite"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7816abf71167","stem":"1) A woman is brought to the ER complaining of vaginal discharge and RUQ abdominal pain. On history, the patient reports having many sexual partners. Pelvic exam reveals cervical motion tenderness, and labs of vaginal discharge detect numerous PMNs but no organisms on Gram stain. The doctor makes a diagnosis based on these findings and administers doxycycline and ceftriaxone. Later, surgeons, concerned about the patient’s abdominal pain, rule out cholecystitis by imaging, but laparoscopy reveals adhesions around the patient’s liver capsule. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen","topicOriginal":"Abdomen","subtopic":"Liver","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia psittaci"},{"label":"B","text":"Clamydia trachomatis"},{"label":"C","text":"Clamydia pneumoniae (TWAR)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c480b82a3cec","stem":"2) A bird shop owner visits his doctor complaining of a headache, fever, and dry cough that has worsened over the last few days. The patient also complains of a sore throat and muscle aches. A physical exam reveals bilateral rales upon auscultation and mild splenomegaly. The doctor orders a CXR that reveals a patchy pneumonitis. Diagnosis is confirmed with serological tests. The patient is administered tetracycline and the fever diminishes within 2 days. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Head","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia psittaci"},{"label":"B","text":"Clamydia trachomatis"},{"label":"C","text":"Clamydia pneumoniae (TWAR)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd228683dbe4","stem":"3) A 22-year-old student presents with a nonproductive cough, fever, and sore throat. CXR demonstrates diffuse interstitial infiltrate. Sputum Gram stain shows many PMNs but no organism, and a Giemsa stain reveals intracytoplasmic inclusions in epithelial cells. Doxycycline treatment is begun. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia psittaci"},{"label":"B","text":"Clamydia trachomatis"},{"label":"C","text":"Clamydia pneumoniae (TWAR)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c81dc5bcfc7d","stem":"1) Which of the following is NOT used for laboratory diagnosis of C. trachomatis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microscopy of Gram-negative rods with no peptidoglycan cell wall"},{"label":"B","text":"Molecular amplification (e.g. PCR)"},{"label":"C","text":"Cell culture using certain cell lines (e.g. nonciliated columnar, cuboidal)"},{"label":"D","text":"Antigen test (e.g. ELISA, fluorescence)"},{"label":"E","text":"Vaginal specimen cytology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-94ee0c2ee34c","stem":"2) C. trachomatis would iodine stain ____ and Giemsa stain ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive; Positive"},{"label":"B","text":"Positive; Negative"},{"label":"C","text":"Negative; Negative"},{"label":"D","text":"Negative; Positive 54 – Herpesviruses"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"3255b42995bd42c76169057558d5c461f285ac93"},{"id":"q-02d4fb00251e","stem":"Which of the following describes the structure of herpes viruses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Double-stranded DNA, icosahedral 162 capsid, enveloped"},{"label":"B","text":"Double-stranded DNA, icosahedral 162 capsid, non-enveloped"},{"label":"C","text":"Single-stranded DNA, icosahedral 162 capsid, enveloped"},{"label":"D","text":"Single-stranded DNA, icosahedral 162 capsid, non-enveloped"},{"label":"E","text":"Single-stranded RNA, icosahedral 162 capsid, enveloped Match the site of latency with the virus:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3d52691c3229","stem":"11) Which of the following uses giant cells with “owl’s eye” intranuclear inclusion bodies for laboratory diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-268c5c0212eb","stem":"12) The Tzank smear, characteristic cytopathologic effects in a scraping of the base of a lesion, is used for laboratory diagnosis of all of the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-030d2fddd197","stem":"13) In which of the following do T cells appear as atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"B","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"E","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a8286d220498","stem":"14) Which of the following has spurious production of an IgM antibody to the Paul- Bunnell antigen (heterophile antibody)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"B","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"E","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-dee614dd70ea","stem":"15) A vaccine (live attenuated) only currently exists for which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-12f5230bf715","stem":"Which virus is classically associated with Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and some Hodgkin lymphomas?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Oncogenic viruses","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"independent_checked","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"checked_explained","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":[{"field":"stem","original":"16) Cancer (Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin disease) is only associated with which of the following?","corrected":"Which virus is classically associated with Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and some Hodgkin lymphomas?","reason":"language_or_captured_footer_repair"}]},{"id":"q-ca4938fce1d7","stem":"1) A 2-year-old child experiences a rapid onset of high fever that lasts for 3 days and then suddenly returns to normal. Two days later, a maculopapular rash appears on the trunk and spreads to other parts of the body. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"},{"label":"F","text":"Herpes lymphotropic virus (HHV-6) and Human herpesvirus 7 (HHV-7)"},{"label":"G","text":"Kaposi sarcoma-related virus (HHV-8)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9798e082a632","stem":"2) A Jewish AIDS patient from Isreal presents to the ER with red-brown raised nodules on the lower limbs, face, and genitalia. Lymphodema is found upon palpation. The patient complains about difficulty eating and dark growths are found along the gum line. Which of the following is the mostly likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"},{"label":"F","text":"Herpes lymphotropic virus (HHV-6) and Human herpesvirus 7 (HHV-7)"},{"label":"G","text":"Kaposi sarcoma-related virus (HHV-8)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-304b416f7231","stem":"3) A 34-year-old kidney transplant patient currently on immunosuppressants complains of shortness of breath and coughing. Physical exam reveals fever and abnormal lung sounds while chest x-ray indicates interstitial infiltrates in the lungs. No cysts are detected on silver stain of bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, ruling out Pneumocysitic carinii infection. The doctor makes a diagnosis after viewing a sample of the patient’s lung tissue, which shows abnormal giant cells. Which of the following is the mostly likely cause?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"},{"label":"F","text":"Herpes lymphotropic virus (HHV-6) and Human herpesvirus 7 (HHV-7)"},{"label":"G","text":"Kaposi sarcoma-related virus (HHV-8)"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option E"],"duplicateStemGroup":"983e7912fb713dc67bbc6172e5ab6233d4ea48d8"},{"id":"q-042b71e1a0f3","stem":"4) A 55-year-old man is hospitalized for a recent onset of high fever, headaches, and sporadic sensations of smelling sausages. Physical exam reveals neck stiffness, prompting the physician to perform a lumbar puncture. CSF values indicate elevated lymphocytes, elevated proteins, and normal glucose. A CT image confirms encephalitis localized to the temporal lobes. A diagnosis is confirmed by PCR of the CSF. The physician begins treatment with acyclovir and informs the patient that he may suffer permanent neurological abnormalities from the infection. Which of the following is the mostly likely cause?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes simplex type 1 (HSV-1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simplex type 2 (HSV-2)"},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"},{"label":"F","text":"Herpes lymphotropic virus (HHV-6) and Human herpesvirus 7 (HHV-7)"},{"label":"G","text":"Kaposi sarcoma-related virus (HHV-8) 60 – Orthomyxoviruses"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe0e8d31080d","stem":"1) Which of the following is NOT true for influenza viruses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Envelope contains hemagglutinin (HA)"},{"label":"B","text":"Envelope contains neuraminidase (NA)"},{"label":"C","text":"Envelope lined with two M proteins"},{"label":"D","text":"Capsid symmetry is pleomorphic (spherical or tubular)"},{"label":"E","text":"Influenza A, B, and C have 8 genomic segments"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d59e8f7e293a","stem":"2) Which of the following is true for influenza viruses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Double-stranded DNA"},{"label":"B","text":"Double-stranded RNA"},{"label":"C","text":"Single-stranded positive-sense RNA"},{"label":"D","text":"Single-stranded negative-sense RNA"},{"label":"E","text":"Not a DNA or RNA virus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5df9dcd158e8","stem":"Which of the following is true in comparing influenza A to influenza B?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A has antigenic drift and shift, affects a mixture of animal and human"},{"label":"B","text":"A has antigenic shift, affects only humans"},{"label":"C","text":"B has antigenic drift and shift, affects a mixture of animal and human"},{"label":"D","text":"B has antigenic shift, affects only humans"},{"label":"E","text":"A has antigenic drift, affects only humans"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a7e67d9d1f6a","stem":"1) Given an influenza virus A/Bangkok/1/79/(H3N2), what does 1/79 stand for?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The type of influenza virus"},{"label":"B","text":"The location it was originally isolated"},{"label":"C","text":"The date it was originally isolated (January, 1979)"},{"label":"D","text":"The virulence ration (one person can infect 79 others)"},{"label":"E","text":"The antigens present"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0468958a4588","stem":"2) Given an influenza virus A/Bangkok/1/79/(H3N2), what does (H3N2) stand for?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The type of influenza virus"},{"label":"B","text":"The location it was originally isolated (latitude, longitude)"},{"label":"C","text":"The date it was originally isolated (January, 1979)"},{"label":"D","text":"The virulence ration (one person can infect 79 others)"},{"label":"E","text":"The antigens present"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option B"],"duplicateStemGroup":"5da69eefa0609295c948db6a624592c20f93dd36"},{"id":"q-c480d40a3d0d","stem":"Influenza virus B ____ accomplish antigenic shift and therefore ____ cause pandemics, unlike Influenza virus A.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can; Can"},{"label":"B","text":"Can; Cannot"},{"label":"C","text":"Cannot; Cannot"},{"label":"D","text":"Cannot; Can"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8263baf6eb71","stem":"How is influenza transmitted?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aerosols from cold, dry air such as outdoors in the winter"},{"label":"B","text":"Aerosols from person-to-person such as from coughing"},{"label":"C","text":"Fecal-oral such as from a cook that does not wash their hands"},{"label":"D","text":"Vector-borne such as from lice"},{"label":"E","text":"Water-borne such as from drinking contaminated fluids"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e8c27f1d5a82","stem":"1) Which of the following is the least likely clinical syndrome associated with influenza viral infections?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"“Seal bark” cough in children (croup)"},{"label":"B","text":"Fever persisting for 3-8 days"},{"label":"C","text":"Otitis media in children"},{"label":"D","text":"Malaise, headache, and myalgia"},{"label":"E","text":"Secondary bacterial pneumonia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-300eb6f28734","stem":"2) Which of the following increases the risk of Reye syndrome, which is acute encephalitis affecting children after acute febrile viral infections?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"Gen